{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 74 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "679d946cbadc2e564d5ec6a313d64f3a218475fed7bd951cc056078817f6d554" :edb/db-before-t 73 :edb/db-after-t 74 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533386 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:43:45.210Z" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186054509 1071 "Given ![](images/07/07c13e831a55b71f20d0528b176ff156792a056c7c9188de233a2bdfe42e7e60.png) find ![](images/e8/e8cb7d81cd1994fca0e49f8489930e7be7efba436e4dabd12987a034733d7220.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186054509 1071 "Given $x^2 + y^2 = \\sin{y}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186054509 1074 "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to ![](images/f3/f321eb1d3d227339cd1ea5506e2aca012c961900c1a4ca6378e2371ccdd6695c.png) assuming that ![](images/7e/7e00fb1efa8367f76f7d901aebcae62996084c2c98e489f9b4ad87a9c6f788b6.png)\n\n![](images/8b/8b0ae15727b08a1ef06a810fb5c5bfb550a1064195632714013a79314cdbddeb.png)\n\nNow we solve the equation for ![](images/bf/bf12c4267d83a00c07e465de18ca07f56f0405db5214119d523c3d48aea663b9.png) as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f2f87a7dd7745cf10ddc7133eb70db335e1d664c7f0a3f4b88becc8e0a5461f6.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186054509 1074 "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$, assuming that $y = y(x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} + y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin y) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin y) \\\\\n2x + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\cos y\\frac{dy}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\cos y\\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n2y\\frac{dy}{dx} - \\cos y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2x \\\\\n(2y - \\cos y)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2x \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\frac{2x}{2y - \\cos y} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{2x}{\\cos y - 2y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186054510 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ for the curve $xy+y^2=2x^2+4$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186054510 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ for the curve $xy+y^2=2x^2+4.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186054510 1074 "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y = y(x)$.\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy + y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{2} + 4) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) + \\frac{d}{dx}(y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{2} + 4) \\\\\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y\\frac{d}{dx}(x) + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 4x \\\\\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 4x \\\\\n(x + 2y)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 4x - y \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{4x - y}{x + 2y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} m &=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\left(\\dfrac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{4\\cdot 1-2}{1+2\\cdot 2} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{2}{5} \\,. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186054510 1074 "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x \\\\ (x+2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nm\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{4\\cdot 1-2}{1+2\\cdot 2}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{2}{5} \\, .\n}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186054511 1071 "The equation $x^2-2x+y^2+4y=5$ represents a circle. The normal line at the point $\\left(0, -5 \\right)$ on the circle intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P$. What are the coordinates of $P$?\n\n![](images/b6/b6c52a76654a4a545d356890d4deb04c74301e4091422a51e84551aeb3ec1152.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186054511 1071 "The equation $x^2-2x+y^2+4y=5$ represents a circle. The normal line at the point $\\left(0, -5 \\right)$ on the circle intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c52a76654a4a545d356890d4deb04c74301e4091422a51e84551aeb3ec1152.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186054511 1074 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(0,-5)$. Remember that the slope of the normal line, $m'$, is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm' = -\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y = y(x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2-2x+ y^2+4y) &=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5) \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2)-\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (2x)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (y^2)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (4y)&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5) \\\\[5pt] 2x-2+2y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}+4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=0 \\\\[5pt] (2y+4)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=2-2x \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} &=\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} m &=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\left(\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{2-2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot (-5)+4} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{2}{-6} \\\\[5pt] &=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\,. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=3\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the normal at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y-y_1 &=m'\\left(x-x_1 \\right) \\\\[5pt] y+5 &=3\\left(x-0 \\right) \\\\[5pt] y&=3x-5 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0$, so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y&=3x-5 \\\\[5pt] 0&=3x-5 \\\\[5pt] x&=\\dfrac{5}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3},\\,0 \\right)\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186054511 1074 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(0,-5).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, $m',$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm' = -\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2-2x+ y^2+4y)\n\t&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5)\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2)-\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (2x)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (y^2)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (4y)&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5)\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t2x-2+2y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}+4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=0\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\n\t(2y+4)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=2-2x\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\t&=\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\n\t\n}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)$ is \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tm\n\t&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)}\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\left(\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)}\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{2-2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot (-5)+4}\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{2}{-6}\\[5pt]\n\t&=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\, .\n\t\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is $m'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=3.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\ty-y_1 &=m'\\left(x-x_1 \\right)\\[5pt]\n\ty+5 &=3\\left(x-0 \\right)\\[5pt]\n\ty&=3x-5\n}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\ty&=3x-5\\[5pt]\n\t0&=3x-5\\[5pt]\n\tx&=\\dfrac{5}{3}\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3},\\,0 \\right).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186059475 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186059475 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186059475 1074 "First, we factor $\\sin^2\\theta$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta})\n$$\n\nThen, we use\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1\n$$\n\nto simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ \\cdot (\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2}) &= \\sin^{2} θ \\cdot 1 = \\sin^{2} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2\\theta\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186059475 1074 "First, we factor $\\sin^2\\theta$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2{\\theta}\\cdot(\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta})\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}\\cos^2{\\theta} = \\sin^2\\theta.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186059476 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta}{\\sin\\theta}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186059476 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta}{\\sin\\theta}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186059476 1074 "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\t=\t\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\cdot (\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta)} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta} = \\cot \\theta.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186059476 1074 "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta} = \\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\cdot (\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta)} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\cos θ \\cdot (\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ))/(\\sin θ) &= (\\cos θ \\cdot (1))/(\\sin θ) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\cos θ}{\\sin θ} \\\\\n&= \\cot θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos \\theta \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^3 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta} = \\cot \\theta\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186059477 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186059477 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186059477 1074 "First, we rewrite the second term using the identity\n\n$$\n\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\dfrac{\\cos{\\theta}}{\\sin{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sin θ \\cdot \\sin θ)/(\\sin θ(1 + \\cos θ)) + (\\cos θ \\cdot (1 + \\cos θ))/(\\sin θ(1 + \\cos θ)) &=  \\\\\n&= (\\sin θ \\cdot \\sin θ + \\cos θ \\cdot (1 + \\cos θ))/(\\sin θ(1 + \\cos θ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos θ + \\cos^{2} θ)/(\\sin θ(1 + \\cos θ)) &=  \\\\\n((\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ) + \\cos θ)/(\\sin θ(1 + \\cos θ)) &=  \\\\\n(1 + \\cos θ)/(\\sin θ(1 + \\cos θ)) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{\\sin θ} &=  \\\\\n&= \\csc θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta} = \\csc\\theta\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186059477 1074 "First, we rewrite the second term using the identity $\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\dfrac{\\cos{\\theta}}{\\sin{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & = \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta} = \\csc\\theta.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186059485 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186059485 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186059485 1074 "First, we rewrite the denominator using\n\n$$\n1-\\cos^2 x = \\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}} = \\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{\\sin^2{x}}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the expression by separating out the factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin^{2} x} &= \\frac{\\cos x \\cdot 1}{\\sin x \\cdot \\sin x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sin x} \\\\\n&= \\cot x\\csc x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}}=\\cot{x}\\csc{x}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186059485 1074 "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-\\cos^2 x = \\sin^2 x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}} = \\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{\\sin^2{x}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the expression by separating out the factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{1-\\cos^2{x}}=\\cot{x}\\csc{x}.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186059486 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186059486 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186059486 1074 "First, we rewrite the numerator using\n\n$$\n1-\\sin^2 x = \\cos^2 x\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = \\dfrac{1-\\sin^2 x}{1-\\sin x}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1 - \\sin^{2} x}{1 - \\sin x} &= ((1 - \\sin x)(1 + \\sin x))/(1 - \\sin x) \\\\\n&= ((1 - \\sin x)(1 + \\sin x))/(1 - \\sin x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + \\sin x}{1} \\\\\n&= 1 + \\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = 1 + \\sin x\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186059486 1074 "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-\\sin^2 x = \\cos^2 x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = \\dfrac{1-\\sin^2 x}{1-\\sin x}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos^2 x}{1-\\sin x} = 1 + \\sin x.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186059492 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186059492 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186059492 1074 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x - \\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms.\n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x -(\\sin^2x + \\cos^2 x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use\n\n$$\n\\sin^2{x} + \\cos^2{x} = 1\n$$\n\nto simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x = 2\\sin x\\cos x - 1\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186059492 1074 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms. \n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x -(\\sin^2x + \\cos^2 x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{x} + \\cos^2{x} = 1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x = 2\\sin x\\cos x - 1.\