 x = 1-\\cos^2 x$ to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, as follows:\n\n$$\n3(1-\\cos^2 x) = 3\\cdot \\sin^2 x = 3\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right)= 3\\sin^2 x.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186064015 1071 "Find the derivative of $y = \\arctan (-7x)$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186064015 1071 "Find the derivative of $y = \\arctan (-7x).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186064015 1074 "The formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\arctan x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\arctan (-7x)) \\\\\n&= (1)/(1 + (-7x)^{2}) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(-7x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 + 49x^{2}} \\cdot (-7) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{7}{1 + 49x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186064015 1074 "The formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\arctan x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-7x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+(-7x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-7x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+49{x}^{2}}\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{1+49{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186064016 1071 "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$, if $y = \\arccos (5-8x)$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186064016 1071 "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ if $y = \\arccos (5-8x).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186064016 1074 "The formula for the derivative of arccosine is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\arccos x) = -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\a