o 0} \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{[\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)]'}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\left(1/x^2\\right)\\cdot 2x}{-\\left({4}/{x^3}\\right)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{x^{-2} \\cdot 2x}{-4x^{-3}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2x^{-1}}{-4x^{-3}} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\lim_{x\\to 0} {x^2} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\[5pt] &= 0. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186068543 1074 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are\n$\\displaystyle\n \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\ln\\left(x^2\\right) = -\\infty$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2}{x^2} = \\infty$ respectively,\nso we have the case $\\dfrac{-\\infty}{\\infty}$.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{[\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)]'}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\left(1/x^2\\right)\\cdot 2x}{-\\left({4}/{x^3}\\right)}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{x^{-2} \\cdot 2x}{-4x^{-3} }\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{2x^{-1}}{-4x^{-3} }\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}  \\lim_{x\\to 0} {x^2}\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\[5pt]\n&= 0.\n}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186068544 1071 "Evaluate ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\frac {x^6}{5x^6-4} }.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186068544 1071 "Evaluate ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\frac {x^6}{5x^6-4}}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186068544 1074 "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}\n$$\n\nso applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {x^6}{5x^6-4} &= \\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\dfrac {[x^6]'}{[5x^6-4]'} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {6x^5}{5(6x^5)-0} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {1}{5} \\\\ &= \\dfrac{1}{5}. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186068544 1074 "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {x^6}{5x^6-4} &= \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\dfrac {[x^6]'}{[5x^6-4]'}\\\n&= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {6x^5}{5(6x^5)-0}\\\n&=  \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {1}{5}\\\n&= \\dfrac{1}{5}.\n}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078214 1071 "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $1.1$ | $1.3$ | $1.7$ | $2.5$ |\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=0.2$ using a central difference approximation." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078214 1071 "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $1.1$ | $1.3$ | $1.7$ | $2.5$ |\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=0.2$ using a central difference approximation." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078214 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=0.2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0.2) & ≈\\frac{f(0.2+0.1)-f(0.2-0.1)}{2(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(0.3)-f(0.1)}{2(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.7-1.1}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078214 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=0.2$. We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.1$. This gives\n\n$$\nf^{′}(0.2) \\mid \\approx (f(0.2 + 0.1) - f(0.2 - 0.1))/(2(0.1)); = (f(0.3) - f(0.1))/(2(0.1)); = \\frac{1.7 - 1.1}{0.2}; = \\frac{0.6}{0.2}; = \\frac{6}{2}; = 3\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078215 1071 "![](images/53/5355859a0307e46ae84d12bc5e0b183d72458972c8a417fb6d9296b4769ea078.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above.\nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=4$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078215 1071 "![](images/53/5355859a0307e46ae84d12bc5e0b183d72458972c8a417fb6d9296b4769ea078.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078215 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\dfrac{g(x+h) - g(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=4$ with step size $h=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4+4)-g(4-4)}{2(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(8)-g(0)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-(-10)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{8} \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078215 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\dfrac{g(x+h) - g(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=4$ with step size $h=4$. This gives\n\n$$\ng^{′}(4) \\mid \\approx (g(4 + 4) - g(4 - 4))/(2(4)); = (g(8) - g(0))/(8); = (-4 - (-10))/(8); = \\frac{6}{8}; = 0.75\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078218 1071 "A car engine is being heated from a temperature of about $-10^{\\circ}$. The temperature of the engine after $t$ minutes is given by $T(t)$. The graph of $T(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe78a78911e2df9cdb488ec57b0e0a34bb56e18b57303d895dc8013b64a4afc3.png)\n\nApproximate the rate at which the temperature of the engine increases when $t = 3$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078218 1071 "A car engine is being heated from a temperature of about $-10^{\\circ}.$ The temperature of the engine after $t$ minutes is given by $T(t).$  The graph of $T(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe78a78911e2df9cdb488ec57b0e0a34bb56e18b57303d895dc8013b64a4afc3.png)\n\nApproximate the rate at which the temperature of the engine increases when $t = 3$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078218 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\nT'(t) \\approx \\dfrac{T(t+h) - T(t-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\nat the time $t=3$ with step size $h=1$. This gives\n\n$$\nT^{′}(3) \\mid \\approx (T(3 + 1) - T(3 - 1))/(2(1)); = (T(4) - T(2))/(2); = \\frac{60 - 30}{2}; = \\frac{30}{2}; = 15\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of temperature increase is approximately $15^{\\circ}$ per minute." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078218 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\nT'(t) \\approx \\dfrac{T(t+h) - T(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=3$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{T(3+1)-T(3-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{T(4)-T(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{60-30}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of temperature increase is approximately $15^{\\circ}$ per minute." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078221 1071 "If $D(y)=y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} - \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} + y^3$, then calculate $D(e^{3x})$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078221 1071 "If $D(y)=y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} - \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} + y^3,$ then calculate $D(e^{3x}).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078221 1074 "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = f_{3}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} +  f_{2}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_3 = y,  f_2 = 0,  f_1 = -1,$ and $f_0 = y^2.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y = e^{3x}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({e}^{x}) & ={e}^{3x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({e}^{3x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x})+{({e}^{3x})}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(3{e}^{3x})-3\\cdot {e}^{3x}+{e}^{9x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{3x})-3\\cdot {e}^{3x}+{e}^{9x} \\\\  & =27{e}^{6x}-3\\cdot {e}^{3x}+{e}^{9x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}({e}^{6x}+27{e}^{3x}-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078221 1074 "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = f_{3}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nf_3 = y, f_2 = 0, f_1 = -1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf_0 = y^2\n$$\n\nTo compute $D(y)$, we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(e^{x}) &= e^{3x}\\frac{d^{3}}{dx^{3}}(e^{3x}) - \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{3x}) + (e^{3x})^{3} \\\\\n&= e^{3x}\\frac{d^{2}}{dx^{2}}(3e^{3x}) - 3 \\cdot e^{3x} + e^{9x} \\\\\n&= e^{3x}\\frac{d}{dx}(9e^{3x}) - 3 \\cdot e^{3x} + e^{9x} \\\\\n&= 27e^{6x} - 3 \\cdot e^{3x} + e^{9x} \\\\\n&= e^{3x}(e^{6x} + 27e^{3x} - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078222 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x^{100}}{e^x}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078222 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x^{100}}{e^x}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078222 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$. However, since $e^x \\gg x^{100}$ for large values of $x$, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x^{100}}{e^x} = 0\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078222 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty.$ However, since $e^x \\gg x^{100}$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x^{100}}{e^x} = 0.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078223 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x}{\\ln{x}}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078223 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x}{\\ln{x}}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078223 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$. However, since $x \\gg \\ln{x}$ for large values of $x$, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x}{\\ln{x}} = \\infty\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078223 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty.$ However, since $x \\gg \\ln{x}$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x}{\\ln{x}} = \\infty.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078224 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{e^{2x}}{\\ln{x}}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078224 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{e^{2x}}{\\ln{x}}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078224 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$. However, since $e^{2x} \\gg \\ln{x}$ for large values of $x$, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{e^{2x}}{\\ln{x}} = \\infty\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078224 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty.$ However, since $e^{2x} \\gg \\ln{x}$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{e^{2x}}{\\ln{x}} = \\infty.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078225 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078225 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078225 1074 "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n\\mid \\cos x \\mid \\leq 1\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $x^2+1$ grows without bound as $x\\to\\infty$. So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1} = 0\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1}\n$$\n\nshown below:\n\n![](images/b4/b48e8a196c5a5ec3b44ec114dcdd4faf85219578c91552925b61cf1c1a65e17f.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078225 1074 "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\cos x| \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $x^2+1$ grows without bound as $x\\to\\infty.$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1} = 0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/b4/b48e8a196c5a5ec3b44ec114dcdd4faf85219578c91552925b61cf1c1a65e17f.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078232 1071 "A spherical balloon is being inflated so that its volume is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing with respect to the radius at the moment when the diameter of the balloon is equal to $12\\, \\text{cm}$.\n\n![](images/e0/e03fb046f74771676d7b1e83a1cf09176adcffaf8112e9de263404b8be74b026.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078232 1071 "A spherical balloon is being inflated so that its volume is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing with respect to the radius at the moment when the diameter of the balloon is equal to $12\\, \\text{cm}.$\n\n![](images/e0/e03fb046f74771676d7b1e83a1cf09176adcffaf8112e9de263404b8be74b026.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078232 1074 "Since we are given that the diameter of the balloon is $12\\, \\text{cm}$, its radius is\n\n$$\nr =6\\, \\text{cm}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that the volume of a sphere with radius $r$ is given by\n\n$$\nV= V(r) = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(r)$ with respect to $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} V'(r) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d r}\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi (3r^2) \\\\[5pt] &= 4\\pi r^2, \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=6$ gives\n\n$$\nV'(6)= 4\\pi (6)^2= 144\\pi\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing when its diameter is $12\\, \\text{cm}\\,$ is $\\, 144\\pi \\, \\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}$.\n\n**Note:** We read $\\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}$ as \"centimeters cubed per centimeter\"." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078232 1074 "Since we are given that the diameter of the balloon is $12\\, \\text{cm},$ its radius is $r =6\\, \\text{cm}.$ Now, we know that the volume of a sphere with radius $r$  is given by \n\n$$\nV= V(r) = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(r)$  with respect to $r$ is \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\nV'(r) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d r}\\left(  \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\right)\\[5pt]\n &  =  \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi (3r^2)\\[5pt]\n &= 4\\pi r^2, \n}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=6$ gives\n\n$$\nV'(6)=  4\\pi (6)^2= 144\\pi.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing when its diameter is $12\\, \\text{cm}\\,$ is $\\, 144\\pi \\, \\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}.$\n\n**Note:** We read $\\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}$ as \"centimeters cubed per centimeter\"." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078233 1071 "Lizzie buys $b$ pounds of bananas at a price $p$, in dollars, that can be modeled by the function $p(b)=5e^{-0.2b}$. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078233 1071 "Lizzie buys $b$ pounds of bananas at a price $p,$ in dollars, that can be modeled by the function $p(b)=5e^{-0.2b}.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078233 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is equal to $p'(5)$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{′}(b) &= \\frac{d}{db}(5e^{-0.2b}) \\\\\n&= 5e^{-0.2b} \\cdot (-0.2) \\\\\n&=-e^{-0.2b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $b=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{′}(5) &= -e^{-0.2(5)} \\\\\n&=-e^{-1} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{e}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{e}\n$$\n\ndollars per pound." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078233 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is equal to $p'(5).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(b) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}b}(5{e}^{-0.2b}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{-0.2b}\\cdot (-0.2) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.2b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $b=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(5) & =-{e}^{-0.2(5)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is $-\\dfrac{1}{e}$ dollars per pound." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078237 1071 "Suppose that $f(-2) = 0$ and $f'(-2)=2$. Estimate the value of $f(-1.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078237 1071 "Suppose that $f(-2) = 0$ and $f'(-2)=2.$ Estimate the value of $f(-1.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078237 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x - (-2)) \\\\\n&= 0 + 2(x + 2) \\\\\n&= 2(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-1.8$. We get\n\n$$\nf(-1.8) \\mid \\approx L(-1.8); = 2(-1.8 + 2); = 2(0.2); = 0.4\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078237 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\  & =0+2(x+2) \\\\  & =2(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-1.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.8) & ≈L(-1.8) \\\\  & =2(-1.8+2) \\\\  & =2(0.2) \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078238 1071 "Let $g$ be a function such that $g'(x) =\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(4) = 3$. Using the tangent line approximation of $g(x)$ at $x=4$, estimate the value of $g(3.6)$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078238 1071 "Let $g$ be a function such that $g'(x) =\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(4) = 3.$ Using the tangent line approximation of $g(x)$ at $x=4,$ estimate the value of $g(3.6).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078238 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $g(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= g(a) + g^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= g(4) + g^{′}(4)(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $g(4)=3$, and since\n\n$$\ng'(x)=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\ng'(4)=\\sqrt{4}=2\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= 3 + 2(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $g(3.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.6$. We get\n\n$$\ng(3.6) \\mid \\approx L(3.6); = 3 + 2(3.6 - 4); = 3 + 2(-0.4); = 3 - 0.8; = 2.2\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078238 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $g(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =g(a)+{g}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =g(4)+{g}^{′}(4)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $g(4)=3,$ and since $g'(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ then $g'(4)=\\sqrt{4}=2.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =3+2(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $g(3.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3.6) & ≈L(3.6) \\\\  & =3+2(3.6-4) \\\\  & =3+2(-0.4) \\\\  & =3-0.8 \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078242 1071 "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-2,-5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0)$. If $f'(-2)=3$, estimate the value of $f(-2.3)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186078242 1071 "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-2,-5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0).$ If $f'(-2)=3$, estimate the value of $f(-2.3)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186078242 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x - (-2)) \\\\\n&= f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(-2,-5)$, so we have\n\n$$\nf(-2)=-5\n$$\n\nAlso, we're 