efore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186087464 1074 "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n$f(tx, ty)|= ((ty))/((tx)); = \\frac{t \\cdot y}{t \\cdot x}; = \\frac{y}{x}; = f(x, y)✓$\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0$. Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1$:\n$f(tx, ty)|= ((ty))/((tx)^{2}); = \\frac{t \\cdot y}{t^{2} \\cdot x^{2}}; = \\frac{1}{t} \\cdot \\frac{y}{x^{2}}; = \\frac{1}{t} \\cdot f(x, y); = t^{-1}f(x, y)\\cdot$\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n$f(tx, ty)|= (ty)/(2(tx))\\sin (((tx)^{2})/((ty)^{2})); = \\frac{t \\cdot y}{t \\cdot 2x}\\sin (\\frac{t^{2}x^{2}}{t^{2}y^{2}}); = \\frac{y}{2x}\\sin (\\frac{x^{2}}{y^{2}}); = f(x, y)✓$\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186087465 1071 "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 9$?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186087465 1071 "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 9?$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186087465 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=9\n$$\n\nrepresents a circle that is centered at the origin, with radius\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt{9} \\\\\nr &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we disregarded the negative solution for the radius since the radius of a circle must be positive.\n\nIn conclusion, the circle is centered at the origin and its radius is $3$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186087465 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is \n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $x^2+y^2=9$ represents a circle that is centered at the origin, with radius\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ r & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we disregarded the negative solution for the radius since the radius of a circle must be positive.\n\nIn conclusion, the circle is centered at the origin and its radius is $3.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [1759218608