 & =6+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding height is\n\n$$\nh={CH} = 4 .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186105548 1074 "Since\n\n$$\nm\\angle{A}=m\\angle{C}\n$$\n\nwe have an isosceles triangle, where $AB=BC$.\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}AB \\cdot BC \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 8^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 64 \\\\\n&= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186105548 1074 "Since $m\\angle{A}=m\\angle{C},$ we have an isosceles triangle, where $AB=BC.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {8}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186105549 1071 "Consider the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle CDE$ shown below. If the area of $\\triangle CDE$ is $25 \\textrm{ cm}^2,$ find the area of $\\triangle ABC.$\n\n![](images/bd/bd9840fd674fe7bf7b4f428591dfc6e9fbbaec8387836ece34694d17eaabe65b.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186105549 1071 "Consider the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle CDE$ shown below. If the area of $\\triangle CDE$ is $25 \\textrm{cm}^2$, find the area of $\\triangle ABC$.\n\n![](images/bd/bd9840fd674fe7bf7b4f428591dfc6e9fbbaec8387836ece34694d17eaabe65b.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186105549 1074 "Let's consider $\\triangle CDE$. We can see that $\\overline{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overline{DE}$ and that\n\n$$\nDE=10\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nSince the area of $\\triangle CDE$ is $25\\textrm{cm}^2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{b \\cdot h}{2} \\\\\n25 &= \\frac{10 \\cdot CD}{2} \\\\\n25 &= 5 \\cdot CD \\\\\nCD &= 5cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overline{CD}$ is also the height of $\\triangle ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overline{AB}$.\n\n![](images/ea/ea7f44b91e87a42029c5f00aae97a2eebf2e472ff890d4b91f4973e1e383e5c7.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $\\triangle ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{b \\cdot h}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{AB \\cdot CD}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6 \\cdot 5}{2} \\\\\n&= 15cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186105549 1074 "Let's consider $\\triangle CDE.$ We can see that $\\overline{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overline{DE}$ and that $DE=10\\textrm{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $\\triangle CDE$ is $25\\textrm{ cm}^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 25 & =\\frac{10\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 25 & =5\\cdot CD \\\\ CD & =5\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overline{CD}$ is also the height of $\\triangle ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overline{AB}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea7f44b91e87a42029c5f00aae97a2eebf2e472ff890d4b91f4973e1e383e5c7.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $\\triangle ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2} \\\\  & =15{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186105566 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}ab\\sin{C}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{C}$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $b$.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= CB = 58 \\\\\nb &= CA = 40 \\\\\nm∠C &= 31^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\sin{31^{\\circ}} = 0.515\\,038\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places, we calculate the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 58 \\cdot 40\\sin 31^{∘} \\\\\n&= 1160 \\cdot (0.515038) \\\\\n&\\approx 597.44\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186105566 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}ab\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{C}$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $b.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =CB=58 \\\\ b & =CA=40 \\\\ m∠C & ={31}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\sin{31^{\\circ}} = 0.515\\,038\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 58\\cdot 40\\operatorname{sin}⁡{31}^{∘} \\\\  & =1160\\cdot (0.515\\,038) \\\\  & ≈597.44,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186105567 1071 "Rounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$?\n\n![](images/bc/bc9f32a9c0e8b042498a72e808a29274bd505e786d8ec43d0e0bc9888fdf6e44.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186105567 1071 "Rounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$\n\n![](images/bc/bc9f32a9c0e8b042498a72e808a29274bd505e786d8ec43d0e0bc9888fdf6e44.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186105567 1074 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle ADC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/ac/acd2e923a65d0821cd59ed8b8274ceb5fc38def896467e37c8b1d454f958699e.png)\n\n First, we find the area for $\\triangle ABC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(42)(35)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{36}^{∘} \\\\  & =735\\operatorname{sin}⁡{36}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈432.022\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $\\triangle ADC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ADC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(20)(18)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{80}^{∘} \\\\  & =180\\operatorname{sin}⁡{80}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈177.265\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD.$ We get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABC}+{A}_{ADC} \\\\  & =432.022+177.265 \\\\  & ≈609\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $609.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186105567 1074 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle ADC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/ac/acd2e923a65d0821cd59ed8b8274ceb5fc38def896467e37c8b1d454f958699e.png)\n\nFirst, we find the area for $\\triangle ABC$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABC} &= \\frac{1}{2}(42)(35)\\sin 36^{∘} \\\\\n&= 735\\sin 36^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx 432.022\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $\\triangle ADC$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ADC} &= \\frac{1}{2}(20)(18)\\sin 80^{∘} \\\\\n&= 180\\sin 80^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx 177.265\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABCD} &= A_{ABC} + A_{ADC} \\\\\n&= 432.022 + 177.265 \\\\\n&\\approx 609\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $609$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186105568 1071 "The area of $ABCD$ is $300\\, \\text{cm}^2$. What is the area of $\\triangle ABC$ rounded to the nearest integer?\n\n![](images/c0/c06ffb123bdd24c3f83419767b6d1578223b8093089b79f91aae8f1fc6121305.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186105568 1071 "The area of $ABCD$ is $300\\, \\text{cm}^2.$  What is the area of $\\triangle ABC$ rounded to the nearest integer?\n\n![](images/c0/c06ffb123bdd24c3f83419767b6d1578223b8093089b79f91aae8f1fc6121305.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186105568 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $\\triangle ACD$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ACD} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot DA \\cdot DC \\cdot \\sin D \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(25)(32)\\sin 70^{∘} \\\\\n&= 400\\sin 70^{∘} \\\\\n&= 375.877... \\\\\n&\\approx 376cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ and $\\triangle ACD$ gives the area of $\\triangle ABC$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{ABC} &= A_{ACD} - A_{ABCD} \\\\\n&= 376 - 300 \\\\\n&= 76cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186105568 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $\\triangle ACD.$ We get \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot DA\\cdot DC\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(25)(32)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =400\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =375.877... \\\\  & ≈376\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ and $\\triangle ACD$ gives the area of $\\triangle ABC.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABC} & ={A}_{ACD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =376-300 \\\\  & =76\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186110374 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(5s + 4)x = 9x\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s?$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186110374 1071 "Consider the equation\n$(5s + 4)x = 9x$\nwhere $s$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s$?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186110374 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 5s + 4, \\qquad b = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5s + 4 = 9.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5s & =5 \\\\ s & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186110374 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = bx$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b$, then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx$. In this case,\n\n$$\na = 5s + 4, \\qquad b = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5s + 4 = 9\n$$\n\nSolving this for $s$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5s &= 5 \\\\\ns &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186110377 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186110377 1071 "Consider the equation\n$(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12$\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r$?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186110377 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c$, and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x$, and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x$, we have\n$k + 4 = 6$.\nSolving this equation for $k$, we get\n$k|= 2$.\n- Equating the constants, we have\n$3r = 12$.\nSolving this equation for $r$, we get\n$r|= 4$.\n\nFinally, since $k = 2$ and $r = 4$, we have\n\n$$\nk + r = 2+4 = 6\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186110377 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n3r = 12.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k = 2$ and $r = 4,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + r = 2+4 = 6.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186110378 1071 "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$\n\n- $-3x = -4x + 6$\n\n- $2x + 7 = 2x - 1$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186110378 1071 "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n1. $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$\n2. $-3x = -4x + 6$\n3. $2x + 7 = 2x - 1$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186110378 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =4x+1 \\\\ 4x+1-4x & =4x+1-4x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x & =-4x+6 \\\\ -3x+4x & =-4x+6+4x \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-3x = -4x + 6$ has the unique solution $x = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+7 & =2x-1 \\\\ 2x+7-2x & =2x-1-2x \\\\ 7 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186110378 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n$4x + 1|= 4x + 1; 4x + 1 - 4x|= 4x + 1 - 4x; 1|= 1$.\nThis equation is true for all values of $x$. Therefore, the equation $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n- For the second equation, we have\n$-3x|=-4x + 6; - 3x + 4x|=-4x + 6 + 4x; x|= 6$.\nTherefore, the equation $-3x = -4x + 6$ has the unique solution $x = 6$.\n- For the third equation, we have\n$2x + 7|= 2x - 1; 2x + 7 - 2x|= 2x - 1 - 2x; 7|=-1$.\nThis equation is false for all values of $x$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186110448 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186110448 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186110448 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 \\theta = \\sec^2{\\theta}\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nWe now cancel the common factor of $\\sec^2 \\theta$ in the numerator and denominator and simplify the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2\\sec^{2} θ}{\\sec^{2} θ} &= \\frac{2\\sec^{2} θ}{\\sec^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot 1}{1} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = 2\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186110448 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 \\theta = \\sec^2{\\theta}\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nWe now cancel the common factor of $\\sec^2 \\theta$ in the numerator and denominator and simplify the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{2\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = 2.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114953 1071 "Calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ if $y = 2 \\,\\textrm {arccot } x.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114953 1071 "Calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ if $y = 2 \\,\\textrm {arccot} x$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114953 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $\\textrm{arccot}$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\textrm {arccot } x) \t=  -\\dfrac 1 {1+x^2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\,\\text{arccot }x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\text{arccot }x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114953 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $\\textrm{arccot}$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\textrm {arccot} x) = -\\dfrac 1 {1+x^2}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2arccotx) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(arccotx) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{1 + x^{2}}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{1 + x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114957 1071 "If $f(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{arcsec } x}{\\textrm{arccsc } x},$ calculate $f'(x).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114957 1071 "If $f(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{arcsec} x}{\\textrm{arccsc} x}$, calculate $f'(x)$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114957 1074 "Here, we let\n\n$$\nu(x) = \\textrm{arcsec} x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nv(x) = \\textrm{arccsc} x\n$$\n\n, and apply the quotient rule.\n\nWe use the formulae\n\n$$\nu'(x) = \\dfrac 1 {\\begin{vmatrix}x & \\sqrt {x^2 - 1}} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v'(x) = -\\dfrac 1 {\\mid x\\end{vmatrix} \\sqrt {x^2 - 1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (u^{′}(x) \\cdot v(x) - u(x) \\cdot v^{′}(x))/([v(x)]^{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}x & \\sqrt{x^{2} - 1}} \\cdot arccscx - arcsecx \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\mid x\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{x^{2} - 1}}))/(arccsc^{2}x) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{\\mid x\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{x^{2} - 1}})\\frac{arccscx + arcsecx}{arccsc^{2}x} \\\\ = \\frac{arccscx + arcsecx}{\\mid x\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{x^{2} - 1}arccsc^{2}x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114957 1074 "Here, we let $u(x) = \\textrm{arcsec } x$ and $v(x) = \\textrm{arccsc } x$, and apply the quotient rule.\n\nWe use the formulae\n\n$$\nu'(x) =  \\dfrac 1 {|x| \\sqrt {x^2 - 1}}\t\\quad \\text{and} \\quad\tv'(x) = -\\dfrac 1 {|x| \\sqrt {x^2 - 1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\text{arccsc }x-\\text{arcsec }x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})}{{\\text{arccsc }}^{2}x} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})\\frac{\\text{arccsc }x+\\text{arcsec }x}{{\\text{arccsc }}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{arccsc }x+\\text{arcsec }x}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}{\\text{ arccsc }}^{2}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114958 1071 "Plot the graph of $y=-2(7^x)-1$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114958 1071 "Plot the graph of $y=-2(7^x)-1.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114958 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=2(7^x)$.\n\n![](images/08/088e67a4b4d49a0e26d01f473e1513bce26c146da4173ada9d6b401f411253cd.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-2(7^x)$. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,-2)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](images/48/48d991aa81c055a890199355e88c28a8ec178411687ebc260eec50e70718a72a.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $1$ unit down to give $y=-2(7^x)-1$. The point $(0,-2)$ moves to $(0,-3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-1$.\n\n![](images/7e/7e44e7df0372c67db670abd63706c316560a226bd3f63dc8f9c0ca104342128c.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114958 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=2(7^x).$\n\n![](images/08/088e67a4b4d49a0e26d01f473e1513bce26c146da4173ada9d6b401f411253cd.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-2(7^x).$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,-2)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/48/48d991aa81c055a890199355e88c28a8ec178411687ebc260eec50e70718a72a.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $1$ unit down to give $y=-2(7^x)-1.$ The point $(0,-2)$ moves to $(0,-3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-1.$\n\n![](images/7e/7e44e7df0372c67db670abd63706c316560a226bd3f63dc8f9c0ca104342128c.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114959 1071 "Plot the graph of $y = -\\left( \\dfrac 12 \\right)^x + 2.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114959 1071 "Plot the graph of $y = -\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)^x + 2$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114959 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}$.\n\n![](images/e9/e9331b26e26b99d3d8fcaea0bb7864b45f697211d3f30c41e9cc5329b1ef5d45.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}$. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](images/df/df29fef3da108d38afc6a0275cc8b0f52237915576a8d7528f90ffeab5588264.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $2$ units up to give $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+2$. The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,1)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=2$.\n\n![](images/e6/e63aa682e19fef317badd419805ffd82a449559a159a41650d2260daf33eff76.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114959 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}.$\n\n![](images/e9/e9331b26e26b99d3d8fcaea0bb7864b45f697211d3f30c41e9cc5329b1ef5d45.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/df/df29fef3da108d38afc6a0275cc8b0f52237915576a8d7528f90ffeab5588264.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $2$ units up to give $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+2.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,1)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=2.$\n\n![](images/e6/e63aa682e19fef317badd419805ffd82a449559a159a41650d2260daf33eff76.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114960 1071 "If $f(x) =3 x\\, \\textrm {arccsc }x,$ calculate $f'(x).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114960 1071 "If $f(x) =3 x\\, \\textrm {arccsc}x$, calculate $f'(x)$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114960 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $\\textrm{arccsc}$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\textrm {arccsc } x) \t=  -\\dfrac 1 {|x| \\sqrt {x^2 - 1}} \\, .\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x\\,\\text{arccsc }x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\,\\text{arccsc }x) \\\\  & =3(x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\text{arccsc }x)+\\text{arccsc }x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)) \\\\  & =3x(-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})+3\\,\\text{arccsc }x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}+3\\,\\text{arccsc }x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114960 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $\\textrm{arccsc}$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\textrm {arccsc} x) = -\\dfrac 1 {\\mid x \\mid \\sqrt {x^2 - 1}} \\,\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(3xarccscx) \\\\\n&= 3\\frac{d}{dx}(xarccscx) \\\\\n&= 3(x \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(arccscx) + arccscx \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x)) \\\\\n&= 3x(-\\frac{1}{\\mid x\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{x^{2} - 1}}) + 3arccscx \\cdot 1 \\\\ =-\\frac{3x}{\\mid x\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{x^{2} - 1}} + 3arccscx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114962 1071 "Find $f'(x)$ if $f(x) =2 \\, \\textrm {arccot } (x^2 + 5).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114962 1071 "Find $f'(x)$ if $f(x) =2 \\, \\textrm {arccot} (x^2 + 5)$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114962 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $\\textrm{arccot}$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\textrm {arccot } x) \t=  -\\dfrac 1 {1+x^2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\,\\text{arccot }({x}^{2}+5)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{1+({x}^{2}+5)^{2}})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{1+({x}^{2}+5)^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4x}{1+({x}^{2}+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4x}{{x}^{4}+10{x}^{2}+26}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114962 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $\\textrm{arccot}$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\textrm {arccot} x) = -\\dfrac 1 {1+x^2}\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2arccot(x^{2} + 5)) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (- (1)/(1 + (x^{2} + 5)^{2})) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} + 5) \\\\\n&= - (2)/(1 + (x^{2} + 5)^{2}) \\cdot 2x \\\\\n&= - (4x)/(1 + (x^{2} + 5)^{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{4x}{x^{4} + 10x^{2} + 26}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114976 1071 "Find the derivative of $\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t)$ with respect to $t$, where $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$f(t)|= (4t^{2} + 9)i - (\\frac{1}{t - 2})j + \\cos 2tk; g(t)|= (4t - 1)i + e^{3t}j + \\cos 2tk$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114976 1071 "Find the derivative of $\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t)$ with respect to $t,$ where $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =(4{t}^{2}+9)\\,i-(\\frac{1}{t-2})\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =(4t-1)\\,i+{e}^{3t}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114976 1074 "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t))' = \\mathbf f'(t) + 2\\mathbf g'(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(4t^{2} + 9)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(-\\frac{1}{t - 2})j + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos 2t)k \\\\\n&= 8ti + (1)/((t - 2)^{2})j - 2\\sin 2tk, \\\\\ng^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(4t - 1)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{3t})j + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos 2t)k \\\\\n&= 4i + 3e^{3t}j - 2\\sin 2tk\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(t) + 2g(t))^{′} &= f^{′}(t) + 2g^{′}(t) \\\\\n&= (8ti + (1)/((t - 2)^{2})j - 2\\sin 2tk) + 2(4i + 3e^{3t}j - 2\\sin 2tk) \\\\\n&= 8(t + 1)i + ((1)/((t - 2)^{2}) + 6e^{3t})j - 6\\sin 2tk\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114976 1074 "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t))' = \\mathbf f'(t) + 2\\mathbf g'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}+9)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{1}{t-2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,k \\\\  & =8t\\,i+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k, \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)k \\\\  & =4\\,i+3{e}^{3t}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f(t)+2g(t))^{′} & ={f}^{′}(t)+2{g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(8t\\,i+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k)+2(4\\,i+3{e}^{3t}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k) \\\\  & =8(t+1)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}+6{e}^{3t})\\,j-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114977 1071 "Find $\\big( u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)'$ where ![](images/9e/9e23d542bfc56d506f5437a35a92c304d618109ef60086e1f5a7a4fee57d8a80.png) and $u(t) = t.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114977 1071 "Find $\\big(u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)'$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}\\ln t \\\\ -t \\\\ e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t) = t$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114977 1074 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\big( u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)' = u'(t) \\mathbf{g}(t) + u(t) \\mathbf{g}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $u'(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)g(t))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)g(t)+u(t){g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+t\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -t \\\\ 2t{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ -2t \\\\ (2t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114977 1074 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)' = u'(t) \\mathbf{g}(t) + u(t) \\mathbf{g}'(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $u'(t)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng^{′}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\ln t) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(-t) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nu^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(u(t)g(t))^{′} &= u^{′}(t)g(t) + u(t)g^{′}(t) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\ln t \\\\ -t \\\\ e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} + t \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\ln t \\\\ -t \\\\ e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -t \\\\ 2te^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\ln t \\\\ -2t \\\\ (2t + 1)e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114978 1071 "Find $(\\mathbf f \\circ u)'(t)$, if $\\mathbf f(u) = \\ln u \\, \\mathbf i + (u^2+5) \\, \\mathbf j + 4u \\, \\mathbf k$ and $u(t) =2t^3$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114978 1071 "Find $(\\mathbf f \\circ u)'(t),$ if $\\mathbf f(u) = \\ln u \\, \\mathbf i + (u^2+5) \\, \\mathbf j + 4u \\, \\mathbf k$ and $u(t) =2t^3.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114978 1074 "By the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f \\circ u)'(t) = \\mathbf{f}'(u(t)) u'(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(u) &= \\frac{d}{du}(\\ln u)i + \\frac{d}{du}(u^{2} + 5)j + \\frac{d}{du}(4u)k \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{u}i + 2uj + 4k, \\\\\nu^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3}) = 6t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f∘u)^{′}(t) &= f^{′}(u(t))u^{′}(t) \\\\\n&= ((1)/(u(t))i + 2u(t)j + 4k)u^{′}(t) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2t^{3}}i + 2(2t^{3})j + 4k) \\cdot 6t^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{t}i + 24t^{5}j + 24t^{2}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114978 1074 "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f \\circ u)'(t) = \\mathbf{f}'(u(t)) u'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡u)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({u}^{2}+5)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(4u)k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\,i+2u\\,j+4\\,k, \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})=6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{u(t)}\\,i+2u(t)\\,j+4\\,k){u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{3}}\\,i+2(2{t}^{3})\\,j+4\\,k)\\cdot 6{t}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+24{t}^{5}\\,j+24{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114979 1071 "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\cdot \\mathbf g)(t)$, given the following functions $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$:\n$f(t)|= \\sqrt{t}i + t^{2}j; g(t)|= \\frac{1}{t}i + e^{t}j$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114979 1071 "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\cdot \\mathbf g)(t),$ given the following functions $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\sqrt{t}\\,i+{t}^{2}j \\\\ g(t) & =\\frac{1}{t}i+{e}^{t}j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114979 1074 "Using the dot product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f} \\cdot \\mathbf{g})(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{g}(t))\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}f(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sqrt{t})i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})j \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}i + 2tj, \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}g(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{1}{t})i + \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t})j \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{t^{2}}i + e^{t}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\cdot \\mathbf g)