ce, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (m∠B) &= \\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\\n\\frac{4}{3} &= \\frac{2x + 2}{2x} \\\\\n4 \\cdot 2x &= 3 \\cdot (2x + 2) \\\\\n8x &= 6x + 6 \\\\\n2x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= 2x + 2 = 2(3) + 2 = 8, \\\\\nBC &= 2x = 2(3) = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}BC \\cdot AC \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114997 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac12 BC \\cdot AC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\tan(m\\angle{B}) = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$ Notice that relative to $\\angle{B},$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overline{AC}$ and adjacent side $\\overline{BC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & =\\frac{2x+2}{2x} \\\\ 4\\cdot 2x & =3\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\ 8x & =6x+6 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =2x+2=2(3)+2=8, \\\\ BC & =2x=2(3)=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}BC\\cdot AC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114998 1071 "Given that $\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac 13$, calculate the area (in square units) of $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3acaffefa93ecbf278a6126661fb9b128ed4f904a22306acd32289eef46d2e0a.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114998 1071 "Given that $\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac 13,$ calculate the area (in square units) of $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3acaffefa93ecbf278a6126661fb9b128ed4f904a22306acd32289eef46d2e0a.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114998 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac12 AC \\cdot BC\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\theta = m\\angle{A}\n$$\n\nNotice we're also given the length of the side $\\overline{BC}$ opposite to $\\angle{A}$. Hence, we can use the sine ratio to find the length of the hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= \\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3} &= \\frac{5}{AB} \\\\\nAB &= 5(3) \\\\\nAB &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the missing side $AC$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= \\sqrt{AB^{2} - BC^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{15^{2} - 5^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{225 - 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{200} \\\\\n&= 10\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}AC \\cdot BC \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10\\sqrt{2} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 25\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114998 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac12 AC \\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac13,$ where $\\theta = m\\angle{A}.$ Notice we're also given the length of the side $\\overline{BC}$ opposite to $\\angle{A}.$ Hence, we can use the sine ratio to find the length of the hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{5}{AB} \\\\ AB & =5(3) \\\\ AB & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the missing side $AC\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\sqrt{A{B}^{2}-B{C}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{15}^{2}-{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{225-25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{200} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =25\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186114999 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac12 AC \\cdot BC\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nm\\angle{B} = 23^\\circ\n$$\n\nNotice that relative to $\\angle{B}$, we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overline{AC}$ and adjacent side $\\overline{BC}$. Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (m∠B) &= \\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\\n\\tan (23^{∘}) &= \\frac{x - 1}{2x} \\\\\n2x \\cdot \\tan (23^{∘}) &= x - 1 \\\\\n1 &= x - 2x \\cdot \\tan (23^{∘}) \\\\\n1 &= x(1 - 2 \\cdot \\tan (23^{∘})) \\\\\nx &= (1)/(1 - 2 \\cdot \\tan (23^{∘})) \\\\\nx &= 6.6203…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= 2x = 2(6.6203…) = 13.2406…, \\\\\nAC &= x - 1 = (6.6203…) - 1 = 5.6203…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}(5.6203…)(13.2406…) \\\\\n&= 37.2080… \\\\\n&\\approx 37.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the one decimal place." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186114999 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac12 AC \\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m\\angle{B} = 23^\\circ.$ Notice that relative to $\\angle{B},$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overline{AC}$ and adjacent side $\\overline{BC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({23}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x-1}{2x} \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({23}^{∘}) & =x-1 \\\\ 1 & =x-2x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({23}^{∘}) \\\\ 1 & =x(1-2\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({23}^{∘})) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{1-2\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({23}^{∘})} \\\\ x & =6.620\\,3…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =2x=2(6.620\\,3…)=13.240\\,6…, \\\\ AC & =x-1=(6.620\\,3…)-1=5.620\