 point $P(x(t),y(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3{cos}^{2}⁡t(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =|3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\ \\text{ } & =|3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =|3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the last step, we were able to remove the absolute value bars because $\\sin{t}>0$ and $\\cos{t}>0$ for $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186119561 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=e^{2t}\\mathbf p(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, if $\\mathbf p(0)=\\mathbf i-\\,\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k$ and $\\mathbf p'(0)=\\mathbf i - \\mathbf j+ 2\\mathbf k$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186119561 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=e^{2t}\\mathbf p(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ if $\\mathbf p(0)=\\mathbf i-\\,\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k$ and $\\mathbf p'(0)=\\mathbf i - \\mathbf j+ 2\\mathbf k.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186119561 1074 "For a curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=x(t)\\mathbf i+y(t)\\mathbf j+z(t)\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nthe unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{2t}p(t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{2t})p(t) + e^{2t}\\frac{d}{dt}(p(t)) \\\\\n&= 2e^{2t}p(t) + e^{2t}p^{′}(t) \\\\\nr^{′}(0) &= 2e^{2(0)}p(0) + e^{2(0)}p^{′}(0) \\\\\n&= 2(i - j - k) + 1(i - j + 2k) \\\\\n&= 3i - 3j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r^{′}(0)‖ &= \\sqrt{(3)^{2} + (-3)^{2} + (0)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 3^{2}} \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(0) &= (r^{′}(0))/(‖r^{′}(0)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(3i - 3j) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i - j) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i - j)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(0) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf i - \\mathbf j)\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186119561 1074 "For a curve $\\mathbf r(t)=x(t)\\mathbf i+y(t)\\mathbf j+z(t)\\mathbf k,$ the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})p(t)+{e}^{2t}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}p(t)+{e}^{2t}{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =2{e}^{2(0)}p(0)+{e}^{2(0)}{p}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =2(i-\\,j-k)+1(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =3\\,i-3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot {3}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(0)}{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(3\\,i-3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is $\\mathbf T(0) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf i -  \\mathbf j).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186119565 1071 "Write down the integral that gives the arc length of the curve  $\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle 3 \\sin t, \\: 2\\sin 2t \\rangle$\nfrom $t =\\dfrac {3\\pi} 2$  to $t = 2\\pi.$\n\n![](images/57/574a16e843a9c81e5fc9ddbbe2c0dc2c308ffcac4991bcbf48c7f45b4871c93b.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186119565 1071 "Write down the integral that gives the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle 3 \\sin t, \\: 2\\sin 2t \\rangle$\nfrom $t =\\dfrac {3\\pi} 2$ to $t = 2\\pi$.\n\n![](images/57/574a16e843a9c81e5fc9ddbbe2c0dc2c308ffcac4991bcbf48c7f45b4871c93b.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186119565 1074 "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(3\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin 2t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3\\cos t, 4\\cos 2t⟩ \\\\\n‖f^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(3\\cos t)^{2} + (4\\cos 2t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9\\cos^{2} t + 16\\cos^{2} 2t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int_{a}^{b}‖f^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{3π/2}^{2π}\\sqrt{9\\cos^{2} t + 16\\cos^{2} 2t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186119565 1074 "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{