y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= \\intQ(x, y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= \\int(xe^{xy} - 13y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= e^{xy} - \\frac{13}{2}y^{2} + g(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = 11x, \\quad f(y) =-\\dfrac{13}{2}y^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\ne^{xy} - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2 + 11x = c\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186147220 1074 "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{11+y{e}^{xy}}{13y-x{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}y = (11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we then rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xe^{xy}-13y)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =11+ye^{xy}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xe^{xy}-13y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(11+y{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}-13y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n \nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (11+y{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =11x+{e}^{xy}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x{e}^{xy}-13y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = 11x, \\quad  f(y) = - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\ne^{xy} - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2 + 11x = c.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186147225 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 2$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186147225 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 2.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186147225 1074 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty)$, the Laplace transform of $f$, denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s)$, is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite.\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\int_{0}^{∞}2e^{-st}dt\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\int_{0}^{∞}2e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= 2lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{0}^{a}e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether\n\n$$\ns \\neq 0\n$$\n\nor $s = 0$.\n\n- If $s \\neq 0$, we have\n$F(s)|= 2lim_(a → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{a}e^{-st}dt; = \\left.2lim_(a → ∞)(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st})\\right|_{0}^{a}; =-\\frac{2}{s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} - 1)$.\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n$lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}s > 0, \\\\ ∞,\\end{vmatrix}s < 0$.\n- If $s = 0$, we have\n$F(0)|= 2lim_(a → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{a}e^{-0t}dt; = 2lim_(a → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{a}dt; = \\left.2lim_(a → ∞)t\\right|_{0}^{a}; = 2lim_(a → ∞)(a - 0); = 2lim_(a → ∞)a; = ∞$.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s = 0$, we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n- For $s > 0$, we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is convergent\n- For $s < 0$, we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s > 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= -\\frac{2}{s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} - 1) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{s}(0 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nF(s) =\\dfrac{2}{s}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s > 0$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186147225 1074 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s \\neq 0$ or $s = 0.$\n\n- If $s \\neq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s = 0,$ we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n\n- For $s > 0,$ we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is convergent\n\n- For $s < 0,$ we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s > 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{s}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=2$ is $F(s) =\\dfrac{2}{s},$ defined for $s > 0.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186147226 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-2t}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186147226 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-2t}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186147226 1074 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty)$, the Laplace transform of $f$, denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s)$, is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite.\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(t)=e^{-2t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st} \\cdot e^{-2t}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st - 2t}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{(-2 - s)t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\