geneous function $f(x,y)$ is $2$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186147248 1074 "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty, tz) = t^nf(x,y, z)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n f(x,y,z)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx, ty, tz) &= (tx)^{3} + 2(ty)^{3} + (tz)^{3} \\\\\n&= t^{3}x^{3} + 2t^{3}y^{3} + t^{3}z^{3} \\\\\n&= t^{3}(x^{3} + 2y^{3} + z^{3}) \\\\\n&= t^{3}f(x, y, z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz) = t^3 f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186147248 1074 "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty, tz) = t^nf(x,y, z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n f(x,y,z)$ for some $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty,tz) & =(tx)^{3}+2(ty)^{3}+(tz)^{3} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}{x}^{3}+2{t}^{3}{y}^{3}+{t}^{3}{z}^{3} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{3}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz) = t^3 f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186147249 1071 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right)$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186147249 1071 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186147249 1074 "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^ng(x,y)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(tx, ty) &= \\frac{1}{tx}\\cos (\\frac{ty}{tx}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{tx}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{t} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= t^{-1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= t^{-1}g(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^{-1} g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $-1$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186147249 1074 "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^ng(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n g(x,y)$ for some $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{1}{tx}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{tx}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^{-1} g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $-1.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186147250 1071 "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+x+y$\n\n- $g(x,y) = x^6-2x^3y^3 + y^6$\n\n- $h(x,y) = 6$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186147250 1071 "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n1. $f(x,y) = x^2+x+y$\n2. $g(x,y) = x^6-2x^3y^3 + y^6$\n3. $h(x,y) = 6$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186147250 1074 "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $2$, but not all the terms have the same degree. The first term has degree $2$, while the other terms have degree $1$.\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $6$. It is a polynomial of degree $6$, and all of its terms are of degree $6$.\n- The function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $0$. It is a polynomial of degree $0$, and all of its terms are of degree $0$. (There is only one term, and it has degree $0$.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186147250 1074 "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $2,$ but not all the terms have the same degree. The first term has degree $2,$ while the other terms have degree $1.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $6$. It is a polynomial of degree $6,$ and all of its terms are of degree $6.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $0$. It is a polynomial of degree $0,$ and all of its terms are of degree $0.$ (There is only one term, and it has degree $0.$)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151908 1071 "Evaluate $f(t) = 9u(t-9)$ at $t=9$, where $u$ is the unit step function." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151908 1071 "Evaluate $f(t) = 9u(t-9)$ at $t=9,$ where $u$ is the unit step function." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151908 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{1, t \\ge 0,; 0, t < 0\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(t)$ at $t=9$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(9) &= 9u(9 - 9) \\\\\n&= 9u(0) \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(9)=9\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151908 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(t)$ at $t=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(9) & =9u(9-9) \\\\  & =9u(0) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(9)=9.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151909 1071 "![](images/fe/fe9901de89c17bccdaa8c0f95440a079e6537dc42fe858fd836d89eb62f29ca7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t)$, where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151909 1071 "![](images/fe/fe9901de89c17bccdaa8c0f95440a079e6537dc42fe858fd836d89eb62f29ca7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151909 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{1, t \\ge 0,; 0, t < 0\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/66/666fe470145e36e88a33d0719268c25226606d4de10e57f1a8e97ccfa4d34eaa.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t)$, we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $2$ units to the left, giving $y=u(t+2)$.\n- Then, we scale $y=u(t+2)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=3u(t+2)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(t)=3u(t+2)\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151909 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/66/666fe470145e36e88a33d0719268c25226606d4de10e57f1a8e97ccfa4d34eaa.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $2$ units to the left, giving $y=u(t+2).$\n\n- Then, we scale $y=u(t+2)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=3u(t+2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=3u(t+2).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151910 1071 "![](images/74/74bc7efebfaa6a798b848d9f0bbc400bc84a727a933fef32069fec6a87e6ed6b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t)$, where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151910 1071 "![](images/74/74bc7efebfaa6a798b848d9f0bbc400bc84a727a933fef32069fec6a87e6ed6b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151910 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{1, t \\ge 0,; 0, t < 0\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y = u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/66/666fe470145e36e88a33d0719268c25226606d4de10e57f1a8e97ccfa4d34eaa.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y = u(t)$ to the given function $y = f(t)$, we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y = u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y = u(-t)$.\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the left, giving $y = u(-(t+1)) = u(-1 - t)$.\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y = 2u(-1 - t)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2u(-1 - t)\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151910 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y = u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/66/666fe470145e36e88a33d0719268c25226606d4de10e57f1a8e97ccfa4d34eaa.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y = u(t)$ to the given function $y = f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y = u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y = u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the left, giving $y = u(-(t+1)) = u(-1 - t).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y = 2u(-1 - t).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t) = 2u(-1 - t).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151911 1071 "Compute the Wronskian $W(y_1, y_2)$ for $y_1(x) =2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151911 1071 "Compute the Wronskian $W(y_1, y_2)$ for $y_1(x) =2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151911 1074 "The Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of two functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ is defined as\n\n![](images/8c/8c9f75d71e362ec7207e00f67adecae7193e35972e46a783faa5aa1ea6c00301.png)\n\nIn our case, we have $y_1(x) = 2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =2x{({x}^{2})}^{′}-{x}^{2}{(2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151911 1074 "The Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of two functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $y_1(x) = 2x$ and\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = x^2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= 2x(x^{2})^{′} - x^{2}(2x)^{′} \\\\\n&= 4x^{2} - 2x^{2} \\\\\n&= 2x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151912 1071 "$$\ny_1(x) = x^2 + 1, \\qquad\ny_2(x) = 2x, \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ above. Given that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, use the Wronskian to explain why the set $\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151912 1071 "$y_1(x) = x^2 + 1, \\qquad y_2(x) = 2x, \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb R$\nConsider the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ above. Given that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, use the Wronskian to explain why the set $\\{y_1(x), y_2(x) \\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151912 1074 "Suppose the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are defined on some interval $I$.\nThe Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of these functions is defined as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I$, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n- If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} + 1 & 2x \\\\ (x^{2} + 1)^{′} & (2x)^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} + 1 & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 1) \\cdot 2 - (2x) \\cdot (2x) \\\\\n&= 2(x^{2} + 1) - 4x^{2} \\\\\n&= 2x^{2} + 2 - 4x^{2} \\\\\n&= 2 - 2x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nTherefore, the set\n\n$$\n\\left\\{y_1(x), y_2(x) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nis linearly independent on $\\mathbb R$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151912 1074 "Suppose the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of these functions is defined as\n![](images/19/19eae90f8f511056865f967ce8bf784261bd0f1b20cec65f478919e4b1c00078.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2x)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2-(2x)\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+1)-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\left\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\right\\}$ is linearly independent on $\\mathbb R.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151913 1071 "Consider the differential equation $x^2y'' - 5x y' + 9y=0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_1(x) = x^3$ is a solution to the equation for $x\\in \\mathbb R$.\n2. $y_2(x) = x^3 \\ln x$ is a solution to the equation for $x > 0$.\n3. The functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151913 1071 "Consider the differential equation $x^2y'' - 5x y' + 9y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x) = x^3$ is a solution to the equation for $x\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\n- $y_2(x) = x^3 \\ln x$ is a solution to the equation for $x > 0.$\n\n- The functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151913 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_1(x){:}$\n$y_{1}^{′}(x)|= 3x^{2}; y_{1}^{″}(x)|= 6x$\nNow, let's substitute $y_1(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n$x^{2} \\cdot 6x - 5x \\cdot 3x^{2} + 9x^{3}|= 0; 6x^{3} - 15x^{3} + 9x^{3}|= 0; 0|= 0✓$\nSo, $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_2(x){:}$\n$y_{2}^{′}(x)|= 3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}; y_{2}^{″}(x)|= 6x\\ln x + 5x$\nNow, let's substitute $y_2(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n$x^{2}(6x\\ln x + 5x) - 5x(3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}) + 9x^{3}\\ln x|= 0; (6x^{3}\\ln x + 5x^{3} - 15x^{3}\\ln x - 5x^{3} + 9x^{3}\\ln x)|= 0; 0|= 0✓$\nSo, $y_2(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- Statement III is true. Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n$W(y_{1}, y_{2})|=\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}; =\\begin{vmatrix}x^{3} & x^{3}\\ln x \\\\ 3x^{2} & 3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}\\end{vmatrix}; = x^{3}(3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}) - x^{3}\\ln x \\cdot 3x^{2}; = 3x^{5}\\ln x + x^{5} - 3x^{5}\\ln x; = x^{5}$\nSince the Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0, \\infty)$, the functions are linearly independent. Hence, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151913 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_1(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_1(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 6x-5x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_2(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x)-5x(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_2(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{5}-3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0, \\infty),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151914 1071 "Given the set of functions $\\{ e^x, \\, \\sin x \\}$ defined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ explain why they are linearly independent." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151914 1071 "Given the set of functions $\\{e^x, \\, \\sin x \\}$ defined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, explain why they are linearly independent." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151914 1074 "Recall that the set of functions $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars $k_1,k_2,\\ldots,k_n$ not all zero such that the linear combination $k_1 \\cdot f_1 + k_2 \\cdot f_2 + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\nk_1 \\cdot f_1(x) + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$ Otherwise, if $k_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that \n\n$$\nk_1 e^x + k_2 \\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{0})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)=0 \\\\  & {k}_{1}\\cdot 1+{k}_{2}\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,{k}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{1})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)=0 \\\\  & e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0\\,⇒\\,e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=0 \\\\ e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{k_1=k_2=0}.$\n\nTherefore, since $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{all}}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{independent}}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151914 1074 "Recall that the set of functions $\\{f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\}$, which are defined on some interval $I$, is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars $k_1,k_2,\\ldots,k_n$ not all zero such that the linear combination $k_1 \\cdot f_1 + k_2 \\cdot f_2 + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2$ is identically zero, i.e,\n\n$$\nk_1 \\cdot f_1(x) + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I$. Otherwise, if\n\n$$\nk_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0\n$$\n\nis the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have that\n\n$$\nk_1 e^x + k_2 \\sin x = 0\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0$, we get\n$k_{1}(e^{0}) + k_{2}(\\sin 0) = 0; k_{1} \\cdot 1 + k_{2} \\cdot 0 = 0⇒k_{1} = 0$.\n- If $x=1$, we get\n$k_{1}(e^{1}) + k_{2}(\\sin 1) = 0; ek_{1} + (\\sin 1)k_{2} = 0⇒ek_{1} + (\\sin 1)k_{2} = 0$.\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{k_{1} = 0; ek_{1} + (\\sin 1)k_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{k_1=k_2=0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{all}}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{independent}}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151915 1071 "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- $y'' = x\\cos(x), \\quad y(2)=7$\n\n- $y'' + 3y = x^2+1, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y(5)=0$\n\n- $2y'' - y' = e^{2x}, \\quad y(-1)=1, \\quad y(1)=3$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151915 1071 "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n1. $y'' = x\\cos(x), \\quad y(2)=7$\n2. $y'' + 3y = x^2+1, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y(5)=0$\n3. $2y'' - y' = e^{2x}, \\quad y(-1)=1, \\quad y(1)=3$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151915 1074 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y$, then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition.\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$).\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=1$)." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151915 1074 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition.\n\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$).\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=1$)." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151916 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0,  \\qquad 1\\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\n$$\ny(1) = 0, \\qquad\ny(3) = -5\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\quad$ The governing differential equation is ![](images/c1/c1e4a45a8051dccc92a71ca2cf0b3851cfb5c6c5c22e2fc416ebcc8aeba96740.png).\n\n$\\quad$ For this BVP, the ![](images/07/077041b590cf1030028330dae6748828deef81c3d8d6a1c70994a90407ac05a2.png) boundary conditions are ![](images/50/50f935ce3a16bbb94697f74737b4bba66dc9ce01d6629e007ff64f46347c1ccb.png)\n\n$\\quad$ The boundary value problem is ![](images/33/339c256afc594fe366f0fdcb008556ef43b33be529fed25c65e7faa077166be2.png)." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151916 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0, \\qquad 1\\leq x \\leq 3$\n\n$y(1) = 0, \\qquad y(3) = -5$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\quad$ The governing differential equation is $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ For this BVP, the $AAAAA$ boundary conditions are $AAAAA$\n\n$\\quad$ The boundary value problem is $AAAAA$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151916 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem,\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b]$, we have the following boundary condition classifications.\n\n- Dirichlet:\n$y(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Neumann:\n$y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Robin (mixed):\n$\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i$, and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\gamma_1 = \\gamma_2 = 0.)\n$$\n\nOtherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f \\equiv 0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n$y''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0$.\nSince the right-hand side $f(x) \\equiv 0$, the equation is $\\textrm{homogeneous}$.\n- The boundary conditions are\n$y(1) = 0, \\qquad y(3) = -5$.\nThese are $\\textrm{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}$.\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151916 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\ny'(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i,$ and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., $\\gamma_1 = \\gamma_2 = 0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f \\equiv 0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\ny''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x) \\equiv 0,$ the equation is $\\textrm{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\ny(1) = 0, \\qquad\ny(3) = -5.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\textrm{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151917 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\ny''(x) + 2y(x) = x,  \\qquad y(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1, \\qquad 0\\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?\n\n- Robin\n\n- Dirichlet\n\n- Neumann\n\n- Periodic" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186151917 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y''(x) + 2y(x) = x, \\qquad y(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1, \\qquad 0\\leq x \\leq 2$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?\n\n1. Robin\n2. Dirichlet\n3. Neumann\n4. Periodic" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151917 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem,\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b]$, we have the following boundary condition classifications.\n\n- Dirichlet:\n$y(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Neumann:\n$y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Robin (mixed):\n$\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Periodic:\n$y(a) = y(b), \\qquad y'(a) = y'(b)$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i$, and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x = a = 0$ and $x = b = 2$.\n\n$$\ny(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nThese are Robin (mixed) boundary conditions." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186151917 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ 