e}^{2\\cdot 3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6} & 2{e}^{6} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n![](images/cf/cfb6a5fdf144808f6d92f84b76b2fed4e0f56a26437d23a2bb241ed11a2aa754.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186157055 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x) = -3x^4 + x^2 + 4x$, show that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186157055 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x) = -3x^4 + x^2 + 4x,$ show that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186157055 1074 "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a$, and we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist ${\\textrm{positive}}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & \\leq K & g(x) &} \\quad\\textrm{for all}\\quad 0 < & x-a\\end{vmatrix} < \\delta\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & \\leq K & x &} \\quad \\textrm{for} \\quad {0 < \\mid x\\end{vmatrix} < \\delta}\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & &= & -3x^{4} + x^{2} + 4x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&\\le\\begin{vmatrix}3x^{4} & + & x^{2} & + & 4x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^{4} + & x & ^{2} + 4 & x\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\mid x \\mid < 1$, then we have ${\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^2 \\lt & x\\end{vmatrix}}$. Also, since $\\mid x \\mid <1$, we have $\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^4 < & x\\end{vmatrix}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^4 \\le 3 & x & \\quad\\text{and}\\quad & x & ^2 \\le & x\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\mid f(x) \\mid \\mid \\le 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^{4} + & x & ^{2} + 4 & x\\end{vmatrix}; \\le 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & + & x & + 4 & x\\end{vmatrix}; = 8 \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & \\leq 8 & x\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhenever $\\mid x \\mid < 1$. Thus, taking ${K=8}$ and\n\n$$\n{\\delta = 1}\n$$\n\nsatisfies the definition, and we conclude that\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(x)\n$$\n\nas $x\\to 0$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186157055 1074 "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist ${\\textrm{positive}}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta$ such that\n\n$$\n{|f(x)| \\leq K|g(x)|}\n\\quad\\textrm{for all}\\quad 0 < |x-a| < \\delta.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{|f(x)| \\leq K|x|}\n \\quad \\textrm{for} \\quad {0 < |x| < \\delta}.\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|-3{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}+4x| \\\\  & \\le |3{x}^{4}|+|{x}^{2}|+|4x| \\\\  & =3|x{|}^{4}+|x{|}^{2}+4|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $|x| < 1,$ then we have ${|x|^2 \\lt |x|}.$ Also, since $|x|<1,$ we have $|x|^4 < |x|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n3|x|^4 \\le 3|x|\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n|x|^2 \\le |x|.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & \\le 3|x{|}^{4}+|x{|}^{2}+4|x| \\\\  & \\le 3|x|+|x|+4|x| \\\\  & =8|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)| \\leq 8|x|$ whenever $|x| < 1.$ Thus, taking ${K=8}$ and ${\\delta = 1}$ satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186157056 1071 "Consider the following statement.\n\n> If $f(x) = e^{x}+ \\sec x$, then $f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$.\n\nA sketch of a proof of this statement is given below\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\; e^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\; \\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(2)$ as $x\\to 0$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\nFill in the blanks in the reasoning below that justify each step.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from lines $XXXXXXXXXX$ and the $XXXXXXX$ property.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from line $XXXXX$ and the $XXXXXXX$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [1759218