xample, $x'_1(t) = 4x_1(t) + 2x_2(t)$ depends on $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$.\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, $x'_1(t) = -3x_1(t)$ depends on only $x_1(t)$ and $x'_2(t) = 7x_2(t)$ depends on only $x_2(t)$.\n- System III is *not* decoupled because, for example, the first equation depends on $x_2(t)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166511 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, $x'_1(t) = 4x_1(t) + 2x_2(t)$ depends on $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, $x'_1(t) = -3x_1(t)$ depends on only $x_1(t)$ and $x'_2(t) = 7x_2(t)$ depends on only $x_2(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because, for example, the first equation depends on $x_2(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166512 1071 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-6{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166512 1071 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations given by\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-6x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3x_{2}(t)$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166512 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n![](images/17/17657d7db880fc0ababc24511039a9d7446952f7521e31ae3351adbb6b28ee61.png)\nthe formula for the general solution is\n![](images/44/44a286f1a4c15598038f38cdb4140534bac24caacb36f32ee4ef9cbe05b3e5e1.png)\n\