aplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{L{x_{1}^{′}(t)} = L{-x_{1}(t) + 4}; L{x_{2}^{′}(t)} = L{2x_{1}(t) - 3x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{sL{x_{1}(t)} - x_{1}(0) =-L{x_{1}(t)} + 4L{1}; sL{x_{2}(t)} - x_{2}(0) = 2L{x_{1}(t)} - 3L{x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{sX_{1}(s) - 1 =-X_{1}(s) + \\frac{4}{s}; sX_{2}(s) - 0 = 2X_{1}(s) - 3X_{2}(s)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nand we have used the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{1\\} = \\dfrac1s\n$$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for $X_1(s)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nsX_{1}(s) - 1 &= -X_{1}(s) + \\frac{4}{s} \\\\\n(s + 1)X_{1}(s) &= 1 + \\frac{4}{s} \\\\\n(s + 1)X_{1}(s) &= \\frac{s + 4}{s} \\\\\nX_{1}(s) &= (s + 4)/(s(s + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for $X_2(s)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nsX_{2}(s) &= 2X_{1}(s) - 3X_{2}(s) \\\\\n(s + 3)X_{2}(s) &= 2X_{1}(s) \\\\\n(s + 3)X_{2}(s) &= 2((s + 4)/(s(s + 1))) \\\\\nX_{2}(s) &= (2(s + 4))/(s(s + 1)(s + 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is\n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\dfrac{s+4}{s(s+1)},\\qquad X_2(s) = \\dfrac{2(s+4)}{s(s+1)(s+3)}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166537 1074 "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{-{x}_{1}(t)+4} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=-L{{x}_{1}(t)}+4L{1} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=2L{{x}_{1}(t)}-3L{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1=-{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-0=2{X}_{1}(s)-3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\},$ and we have used the fact that $\\mathcal L\\{1\\} = \\dfrac1s.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for $X_1(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1 & =-{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =1+\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{s+4}{s} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{s+4}{s(s+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for $X_2(s),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s) & =2{X}_{1}(s)-3{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s+3){X}_{2}(s) & =2{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ (s+3){X}_{2}(s) & =2(\\frac{s+4}{s(s+1)}) \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{2(s+4)}{s(s+1)(s+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\dfrac{s+4}{s(s+1)} ,\\qquad \nX_2(s) = \\dfrac{2(s+4)}{s(s+1)(s+3)}.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166538 1071 "![](images/a6/a699a4e262534557453aaa9e44d1b273dd4489190e50cec8f9ef9e0da91e7d29.png)\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_1(t) \\right\\} = -\\dfrac{1}{s+2} + \\dfrac{2}{s-1} - \\dfrac{2}{s}, \\qquad\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  x_2(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s+2} + \\dfrac{3}{s}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166538 1071 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 3x_{2}(t) - 7; x_{2}^{′}(t) =-2x_{2}(t) + 6$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution\n$X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\} = -\\dfrac{1}{s+2} + \\dfrac{2}{s-1} - \\dfrac{2}{s}, \\qquad X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s+2} + \\dfrac{3}{s}$.\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166538 1074 "To find $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to $X_1(s)$ and $X_2(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= L^{-1}{X_{1}(s)} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{s + 2} + \\frac{2}{s - 1} - \\frac{2}{s}} \\\\\n&=-L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 2}} + 2L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 1}} - 2L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\\n&=-e^{-2 \\cdot t} + 2e^{1 \\cdot t} - 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 2e^{t} - e^{-2t} - 2 \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= L^{-1}{X_{2}(s)} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 2} + \\frac{3}{s}} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 2}} + 3L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\\n&= e^{-2 \\cdot t} + 3 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= e^{-2t} + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}(t) \\\\ x_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2e^{t} - e^{-2t} - 2 \\\\ e^{-2t} + 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166538 1074 "To find $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to $X_1(s)$ and $X_2(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{1}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{s-1}-\\frac{2}{s}} \\\\  & =-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+2\\,{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}}-2\\,{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2\\cdot t}+2{e}^{1\\cdot t}-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}-2 \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{2}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{3}{s}} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+3\\,{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2\\cdot t}+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={e}^{-2t}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}-2 \\\\ {e}^{-2t}+3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166539 1071 "![](images/d8/d8be54eddd0358742170a03b3c55e0cb94eaf2a7d59f12e1c4361a6197d29847.png)\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\ln 2?$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166539 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\ln 2$?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166539 1074 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\ln 2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(\\ln 2) &= e^{3(\\ln 2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + 2e^{\\ln 2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + 2 \\cdot 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(\\ln 2) &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2(12) + 1(4) \\\\ 1(12) + 2(4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}24 + 4 \\\\ 12 + 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166539 1074 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\ln 2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot 2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(12)+1(4) \\\\ 1(12)+2(4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24+4 \\\\ 12+8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166540 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166540 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = 3x(t) - y(t); y^{′}(t) =-2x(t) - y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166540 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which $x' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n$3x-y=0 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad y = 3x$\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which $y' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n$-2x-y=0 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad y = -2x$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/66/66cdd05ac68d762c5b78df40b22f9e0cd660099c13e48e9eb71c81c73a044280.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166540 1074 "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which $x' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n3x-y=0 \n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\ny = 3x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which $y' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n-2x-y=0 \n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\ny = -2x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/66/66cdd05ac68d762c5b78df40b22f9e0cd660099c13e48e9eb71c81c73a044280.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166542 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](images/04/040ac76a28687db1c99d3379242b726a85aa4baf269a7c6d35d9662af1d3bd78.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166542 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = 4x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)$\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](images/04/040ac76a28687db1c99d3379242b726a85aa4baf269a7c6d35d9662af1d3bd78.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166542 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/2e/2ee58da2733a468406869e43f57deebc6f7924f1d1149c0ca9391ea9995d9f87.png)\nwhere ![](images/83/83596c7f3038e585eb35ffafc966810e6891b55fc4d1efb04494bdc4b3755b05.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/af/af5b69c599a13a707365e88f6f50939064c7b199a1d8231b32a1c88e425440fa.png)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/83/833ce06c0c6d5f9beea87a84ef1b286748472c312987e837ca6f47f60225b1fa.png)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/fb/fb015d0935718dd6ffdfb9eb13b2b8fd0c255c63a2f5cecdb988a3f83df14685.png)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/03/03f2711cb5a80e1e94f879bf8a68c25db27f8280f5e1e344425f9ee2a334a829.png)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{4t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/bd/bd4d36cd68896e6360317a0c01c92841423020218436d85ae07eb413463fffa8.png)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-6t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/91/91c074766b92790a0e78b768b0290d7723617d6ed30e51e842501cad63e58a44.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166542 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{4t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-6t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/91/91c074766b92790a0e78b768b0290d7723617d6ed30e51e842501cad63e58a44.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166543 1071 "![](images/25/25d4f77a3f596202229581e06d88a9e3bdd84dc6b8b7e14b6f46b3b3d2f125d2.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166543 1071 "![](images/25/25d4f77a3f596202229581e06d88a9e3bdd84dc6b8b7e14b6f46b3b3d2f125d2.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?\n\n1. ${x^{′}(t) =-5x(t); y^{′}(t) = 6y(t)$\n2. ${x^{′}(t) = 5x(t); y^{′}(t) = 6y(t)$\n3. ${x^{′}(t) = 5x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)$\n4. ${x^{′}(t) =-5x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166543 1074 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/34/343f6f32ac21a1bf06b86174734dd9ebba6ddb7598cbe83b4ade59f70e8e8b26.png)\nwhere ![](images/cb/cb0f32e55af289452f1429ddfe3214a46501c55f3a61deaf10b4f736e47dca7f.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/85/85fdb4fc83a6f2b44178be2e97a0e7985d97350deb4e0fb123e513a80090906a.png)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/3f/3f984a493e976bd522455395defcb4c1562d61ca56e19fa27e2272832ce6011c.png)\nmust approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 < 0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/44/44f82894493c95c38039bc1e6a842bb7226571e9818e3c10c50d33627464ea37.png)\nmust also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 < 0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies $\\lambda_1 < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 < 0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166543 1074 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}λ_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & λ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, we must have $\\lambda_1 < 0$.\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, we must have $\\lambda_2 < 0$.\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies $\\lambda_1 < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 < 0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) =-5x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166544 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = -\\sin x, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = \\cos x,$ compute the local truncation error $\\tau_{6}$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $\\Delta x=0.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166544 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = -\\sin x, \\qquad y(0) = 1$\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following method:\n$Δy|= y^{′} \\cdot Δx; y_{new}|= y + Δy$\nGiven that the exact solution is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = \\cos x$, compute the local truncation error $\\tau_{11}$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $\\Delta x=0.2$. Round your answer to three decimal places." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166544 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x)$ to\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nτ_{n} &= y_{exact}(x_{new}) - y_{new} \\\\\n&= y_{exact}(x_{new}) - (y_{exact}(x) + Δy)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution is\n\n$$\ny_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = \\cos x\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is\n\n$$\ny_{\\textrm{exact}}(1)=\\cos(1)\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{exact}^{′}(1) &= -\\sin (1) \\\\\n&=-0.8415\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{exact}^{′}(1) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&=-0.8415 \\cdot 0.2 \\\\\n&\\approx - 0.1683\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at $x_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{exact}(x_{new}) &= y_{exact}(x + Δx) \\\\\n&= y_{exact}(1 + 0.2) \\\\\n&= y_{exact}(1.2) \\\\\n&= \\cos (1.2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nτ_{11} &= \\cos (1.2) - (\\cos (1) + Δy) \\\\\n&= \\cos (1.2) - (\\cos (1) - 0.1683) \\\\\n&\\approx - 0.010\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166544 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x)$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = \\cos x.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(1)=\\cos(1).$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1) \\\\  & =-0.8415.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-0.8415\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & ≈-0.1683.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at $x_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.2) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.2)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)+Δy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.2)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-0.1683) \\\\  & ≈-0.010\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166545 1071 "An initial value problem has the exact solution $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = \\cos(2x)$. A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and $\\tau_n$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value.\n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.925$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.692$ |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.368$ |" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166545 1071 "An initial value problem has the exact solution $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = \\cos(2x).$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and $\\tau_n$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.925$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.692$ |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.368$ |" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166545 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x)$ to\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nτ_{n} &= y_{exact}(x_{new}) - y_{new} \\\\\n&= y_{exact}(x_{new}) - (y_{exact}(x) + Δy)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have $y_\\text{new} = 0.925$. So, the LTE of the first step is\n$τ_{1}|= y_{exact}(0.2) - 0.925; = \\cos (0.4) - 0.925; \\approx - 0.004$.\n- After the second step, we have $y_\\text{new} = 0.692$. So, the LTE of the second step is\n$τ_{2}|= y_{exact}(0.4) - 0.692; = \\cos (0.8) - 0.692; \\approx 0.005$.\n- After the third step, we have $y_\\text{new} = 0.368$. So, the LTE of the third step is\n$τ_{3}|= y_{exact}(0.6) - 0.368; = \\cos (1.2) - 0.368; \\approx - 0.006$.\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | $\\tau_n$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.925$ | $-0.004$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.692$ | $0.005$ |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.368$ | $-0.006$ |" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166545 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x)$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have $y_\\text{new} = 0.925.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.2)-0.925 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.4)-0.925 \\\\  & ≈-0.004.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have $y_\\text{new} = 0.692.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.4)-0.692 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.8)-0.692 \\\\  & ≈0.005.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have $y_\\text{new} = 0.368.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.6)-0.368 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.2)-0.368 \\\\  & ≈-0.006.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | $\\tau_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.925$ | $-0.004$ |\n|