ses through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y=-x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/31/317fe507f591931ea371db43b78a83b1741c427ef89dd90571f0a0030ed70c20.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n$\\lambda_2 < \\lambda_1 < 0$\nthus,\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = -4\n$$\n\nis the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = -4\n$$\n\nsolutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/3a/3a6d8a97c6d07c78562c73dbd1abef1b1a3c0d9f38cd14f4f9874df091492b9e.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166548 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](images/47/470210fb9509e87c3e51a990bac7483e5862d2f37afdce9823279838e8391592.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186166548 1071 "${x^{′}(t) =-x(t); y^{′}(t) =-4y(t)$\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](images/47/470210fb9509e87c3e51a990bac7483e5862d2f37afdce9823279838e8391592.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186166548 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/70/703fb7adc0ca076e42aecc781d4d0dc58eb5f4fec03db4d18bf5a36f7055a349.png)\nwhere ![](images/72/7210186106fc202e3b9a35ffa32b06b09493ce28c191a89c2824a35a547febff.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/f2/f292220b15ff9b0d695cb8b9f2349cc275557fd2b77c32c147e253