tor field $\\mathbf F = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O$, as shown below. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186179490 1071 "![](images/05/05c91d681a0c84bbe9d8b6cab9bd8cdf1dca8bf88c851b8c7a7045f73ff9844c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186179490 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides $C_2, C_3,$ and $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 4\\cdot 4 = 16.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186179490 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side $C_1$. We have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_{1}}F \\cdot n_{1}ds &= \\int_{C_{1}}(2xi + 2yj) \\cdot ids \\\\\n&= 2\\int_{C_{1}}xds\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1]\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\int_{C_{1}}xds &= 2\\int_{-1}^{1}1 \\cdot \\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid dt \\\\\n&= 2\\int_{-1}^{1}dt \\\\\n&= 2(1 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides $C_2, C_3$, and $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 4\\cdot 4 = 16\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186179491 1071 "![](images/59/59b5f1676f036f80e6aa2e9e331327ed9ec28e88bacda52a91ee0865736e9ce3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 5x\\,\\mathbf i - 3y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186179491 1071 "![](images/59/59b5f1676f036f80e6aa2e9e331327ed9ec28e88bacda52a91ee0865736e9ce3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 5x\\,\\mathbf i - 3y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$ \n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186179491 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/79/793755cda5243dbb427d8722c21ab150a4821c3957f0ccd0ef0fedbbd576e924.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(5x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 2\\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =5{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 20.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2 = \\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(5x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = t\\,\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf i, \\qquad t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = -12.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s= 2 \\cdot 20 + 2 \\cdot (-12) = 16.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186179491 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/79/793755cda5243dbb427d8722c21ab150a4821c3957f0ccd0ef0fedbbd576e924.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1$. We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i$, and therefore,\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{1}}F \\cdot n_{1}ds|= \\displaystyle \\int_{C_{1}}(5xi - 3yj) \\cdot ids; = 5\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{1}}xds$.\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1$, we have\n$\\mathbf r(t) = 2\\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1]$.\nTherefore,\n$5\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{1}}xds|= 5\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}2 \\cdot \\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid dt; = 10\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}dt; = 10 \\cdot 2; = 20$.\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 20$.\n\n- Next, we consider the side $C_2$. We have that $\\mathbf n_2 = \\mathbf j$, and therefore,\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{2}}F \\cdot n_{2}ds|= \\displaystyle \\int_{C_{2}}(5xi - 3yj) \\cdot jds; =-3\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{2}}yds$.\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2$, we have\n$\\mathbf r(t) = t\\,\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf i, \\qquad t\\in [-2,2]$.\nTherefore,\n$-3\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{2}}yds|=-3\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2}1 \\cdot \\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid dt; =-3\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2}dt; = (-3) \\cdot 4; =-12$.\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = -12$.\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s= 2 \\cdot 20 + 2 \\cdot (-12) = 16\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186179496 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation $5^{x+2}=9^{x-2}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186179496 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation $5^{x+2}=9^{x-2}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186179496 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm\n\n$$\n\\left(\\ln\\right)\n$$\n\nof both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{x + 2} &= 9^{x - 2} \\\\\n\\ln (5^{x + 2}) &= \\ln (9^{x - 2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(x+2)\\ln(5) = (x-2)\\ln(9)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 2)\\ln (5) &= (x - 2)\\ln (9) \\\\\nx\\ln (5) + 2\\ln (5) &= x\\ln (9) - 2\\ln (9) \\\\\nx\\ln (5) - x\\ln (9) &= -2\\ln (9) - 2\\ln (5) \\\\\nx\\ln (9) - x\\ln (5) &= 2\\ln (9) + 2\\ln (5) \\\\\nx(\\ln (9) - \\ln (5)) &= 2(\\ln (5) + \\ln (9)) \\\\\nx &= (2(\\ln (5) + \\ln (9)))/(\\ln (9) - \\ln (5))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186179496 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x+2} & ={9}^{x-2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x+2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{x-2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(x+2)\\ln(5) = (x-2)\\ln(9).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) & =2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) \\\\ x & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9))}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186179500 1071 "In a ballet school, the ratio of the number of students with black hair to the total number of students is $5: 8$. If there are $25$ students with black hair, how many students are there in ballet school who don't have black hair?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186179500 1071 "In a ballet school, the ratio of the number of students with black hair to the total number of students is $5: 8.$ If there are $25$ students with black hair, how many students are there in ballet school who don't have black hair?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186179500 1074 "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}8}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}}{{\\color{red}8}}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of students with black hair and the denominator represents the total number of students.\n\nWe know that there are $25$ students with black hair, so we want to make a numerator of $25$. We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}\\times 5}{{\\color{red}8}\\times 5} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}25}}{{\\color{red}40}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since there are $25$ students with black hair, there must be $40$ students at the ballet school. Of this amount, the number of students who do not have black hair is\n\n$$\n40 - 25 = 15\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186179500 1074 "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}8}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}}{{\\color{red}8}}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of students with black hair and the denominator represents the total number of students.\n\nWe know that there are $25$ students with black hair, so we want to make a numerator of $25.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}\\times 5}{{\\color{red}8}\\times 5} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}25}}{{\\color{red}40}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since there are $25$ students with black hair, there must be $40$ students at the ballet school. Of this amount, the number of students who do not have black hair is\n\n$$\n40 - 25 = 15.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186