85 false] [17592186183967 1071 "Rewrite the expression \n\n$$\n\\left(x^2 + 7x + \\dfrac{49}{4}\\right) + 3\n$$\n\nas the sum of a perfect square and a constant." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186183967 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 7x + \\frac{49}{4} &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 7x + (\\frac{7}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x + (\\frac{7}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x + \\frac{7}{2})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} + 7x + \\frac{49}{4}) + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= (x + \\frac{7}{2})^{2} + 3 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186183967 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+7x+\\frac{49}{4} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+7x+\\frac{49}{4})+3 & = \\\\ {(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186183968 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 7x - 1$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186183968 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 7x - 1.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186183968 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwriting the constant term ${\\color{purple}{-1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\dfrac{53}{4}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186183968 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{-1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x  + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\dfrac{53}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186183969 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 7x$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186183969 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 7x.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186183969 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\dfrac{49}{4}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186183969 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\dfrac{49}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186188206 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1 & ={tan}^{2}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)+1 \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (1)+1 \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡x+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x\\sec^2 x - \\tan^4 x +1 = \\sec^2 x.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186188206 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1) & 1 + \\tan^{2} x = \\sec^{2} x \\\\ (2) & \\tan^{2} x = \\sec^{2} x - 1 \\\\ (3) & \\sec^{2} x - \\tan^{2} x = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan^{2} x\\sec^{2} x - \\tan^{4} x + 1 &= \\tan^{2} x(\\sec^{2} x - \\tan^{2} x) + 1 \\\\\n&= \\tan^{2} x \\cdot (1) + 1 \\\\\n&= \\tan^{2} x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan^{2} x + 1 &= \\sec^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x\\sec^2 x - \\tan^4 x +1 = \\sec^2 x\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186198233 1071 "Find the derivative of  $\\mathbf f(t) = \\langle \\sin t ,\\cos t , t \\rangle$ at $t= \\dfrac{\\pi}4.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186198233 1071 "Find the derivative of $\\mathbf f(t) = \\langle \\sin t,\\cos t, t \\rangle$ at $t= \\dfrac{\\pi}4$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186198233 1074 "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\cos t,-\\sin t, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}4\\right)\n$$\n\nwe substitute\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}4\n$$\n\ninto the expression for $\\mathbf f'(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= ⟨\\cos (\\frac{π}{4}),-\\sin (\\frac{π}{4}), 1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186198233 1074 "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute $\\mathbf f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}4\\right),$ we substitute $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}4$ into the expression for $\\mathbf f'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203769 1071 "Find a general expression describing all of the zeros of $y = \\sin\\left( \\dfrac x 2\\right).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203769 1071 "Find a general expression describing all of the zeros of $y = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac x 2\\right)$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186203769 1074 "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX =  \\dfrac x 2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=\\sin X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX =n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =2n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x =2n\\pi,$ where $n$ is any integer." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203769 1074 "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX = \\dfrac x 2\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of\n\n$y=\\sin X$.\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n$X =n\\pi$\nwhere $n