_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-x-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-x-2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy-1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](images/c3/c3ec90119193ca9c0cb886bc7b7c698eb155e6a801fd267c0a17a0d329eff84d.png)\n\nStarting with ![](images/ef/eff9e5b8ab46002bd82fa42d59f60b1bb78ca04228d7c73b52d131d222ccb894.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2xy-1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-x-2 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-y+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1)(2)-1 & (1)^{2} \\\\ (2)^{2} & 2(1)(2)-1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}(2)-1-2 \\\\ 1(2)^{2}-2+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.2 \\\\ -0.6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203831 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}y - x - 2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}y - x - 2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy^{2} - y + 1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy^{2} - y + 1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2xy - 1 & x^{2} \\\\ y^{2} & 2xy - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2xy - 1 & x^{2} \\\\ y^{2} & 2xy - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\begin{bmatrix}2xy - 1 & x^{2} \\\\ y^{2} & 2xy - 1\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2}y - x - 2 \\\\ xy^{2} - y + 1\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(2) - 1 & (1)^{2} \\\\ (2)^{2} & 2(1)(2) - 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2}(2) - 1 - 2 \\\\ 1(2)^{2} - 2 + 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{3 \\cdot 3 - 4 \\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{11}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2.2 \\\\ -0.6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186203832 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3] \\times [-2,0] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + y - 4 \\\\ x + y^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 0 \\big]^T$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $\\mathbf{x}_1 = \\big[3, \\: -1 \\big]^T$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed)." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186203832 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the functio