ned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203838 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x^{2}y - 2x^{2} + 3xy^{2} + 3xy + 1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x^{2}y - 2x^{2} + 3xy^{2} + 3xy + 1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4xy - 4x + 3y^{2} + 3y \\\\ 2x^{2} + 6xy + 3x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy - 4x + 3y^{2} + 3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x^{2} + 6xy + 3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy - 4x + 3y^{2} + 3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x^{2} + 6xy + 3x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4y - 4 & 4x + 6y + 3 \\\\ 4x + 6y + 3 & 6x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4y - 4 & 4x + 6y + 3 \\\\ 4x + 6y + 3 & 6x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\begin{bmatrix}4y - 4 & 4x + 6y + 3 \\\\ 4x + 6y + 3 & 6x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4xy - 4x + 3y^{2} + 3y \\\\ 2x^{2} + 6xy + 3x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4(1) - 4 & 4(1) + 6(1) + 3 \\\\ 4(1) + 6(1) + 3 & 6(1)\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4(1)(1) - 4(1) + 3(1)^{2} + 3(1) \\\\ 2(1)^{2} + 6(1)(1) + 3(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 6\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{0 \\cdot 6 - 13 \\cdot 13}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\