ac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](images/ff/ff3b9d25f1fe5ae3a7f784980aa6bce1340ea9186537f097222fbea82a596257.png)\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6(1.5)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}-3 \\\\ 3(1.5)^{2}-6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 9-0\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{54}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4.5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.417\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203839 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{3} + y^{3} - 3x - 6y + 9) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{3} + y^{3} - 3x - 6y + 9)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 3 \\\\ 3y^{2} - 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x^{2} - 3) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y^{2} - 6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x^{2} - 3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y^{2} - 6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} - \\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{1}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 3 \\\\ 3y^{2} - 6\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6(1.5)\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3(1)^{2} - 3 \\\\ 3(1.5)^{2} - 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{6 \\cdot 9 - 0 \\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{54}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.417\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186203840 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times[0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$f(x,y) = xy^2+3x^2-xy-x$.\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[1, \\: 1 \\big]^T$, and compute the components of the first iteration" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186203840 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times[0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = xy^2+3x^2-xy-x.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[ 1, \\: 1 \\big]^T,$ and compute the components of the first iteration" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203840 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nH_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+6x-y-1 \\\\ 2xy-x\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+6x-y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+6x-y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2y-1 \\\\ 2y-1 & 2x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n![](images/dd/ddb94c6d067c419b06cdbec87426e70db6ee07acca7d42c0900ae386f6fe37ae.png)\n\nStarting with ![](images/8b/8bdb55b26b462507d9810675499558be66e7b970d2f9fdb78e708807e617ce8d.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2y-1 \\\\ 2y-1 & 2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+6x-y-1 \\\\ 2xy-x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & 2(1)-1 \\\\ 2(1)-1 & 2(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}+6(1)-(1)-1 \\\\ 2(1)(1)-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get ![](images/da/daee26a8f82011d916bf04d7e374a2b8f471602877fe76335f7f1b242ca86b00.png) Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.182 \\\\ 0.909\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203840 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nH_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy^{2} + 3x^{2} - xy - x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy^{2} + 3x^{2} - xy - x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}y^{2} + 6x - y - 1 \\\\ 2xy - x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y^{2} + 6x - y - 1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy - x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2} + 6x - y - 1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy - x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y - 1 \\\\ 2y - 1 & 2x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y - 1 \\\\ 2y - 1 & 2x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y - 1 \\\\ 2y - 1 & 2x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= -\\begin{bmatrix}y^{2} + 6x - y - 1 \\\\ 2xy - x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2(1) - 1 \\\\ 2(1) - 1 & 2(1)\\end{bmatrix}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= -\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2} + 6(1) - (1) - 1 \\\\ 2(1)(1) - 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $\\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{bmatrix}$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} + \\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}0.182 \\\\ 0.909\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186203858 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$\n\n- $2z - 5 = z + 1$\n\n- $3z - 2 = 3z - 2$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203858 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n1. $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$\n2. $2z - 5 = z + 1$\n3. $3z - 2 = 3z - 2$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186203858 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z+3 & =4z+9 \\\\ 4z+3-4z & =4z+9-4z \\\\ 3 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $z.$ Therefore, the equation $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-5 & =z+1 \\\\ 2z-5-z+5 & =z+1-z+5 \\\\ z & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2z - 5 = z + 1$ has the unique solution $z = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-2 & =3z-2 \\\\ 3z-2-3z & =3z-2-3z \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203858 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n$4z + 3|= 4z + 9; 4z + 3 - 4z|= 4z + 9 - 4z; 3|= 9$.\nThis equation is false for all values of $z$. Therefore, the equation $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$ has no solutions.\n- For the second equation, we have\n$2z - 5|= z + 1; 2z - 5 - z + 5|= z + 1 - z + 5; z|= 6$.\nTherefore, the equation $2z - 5 = z + 1$ has the unique solution $z = 6$.\n- For the third equation, we have\n$3z - 2|= 3z - 2; 3z - 2 - 3z|= 3z - 2 - 3z; - 2|=-2$.\nThis equation is true for all values of $z$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186203859 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $y$, given by\n$4(r - 2)y = -8$\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r$?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186203859 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n4(r - 2)y = -8\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203859 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay = b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 4(r - 2), \\qquad b = -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4(r - 2) = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(r-2) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4(r-2)}{4} & =\\frac{0}{4} \\\\ r-2 & =0 \\\\ r & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203859 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = b$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n$$\nb\\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf $a = 0$, then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay = b$. In this case,\n\n$$\na = 4(r - 2), \\qquad b = -8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4(r - 2) = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(r - 2) &= 0 \\\\\n(4(r - 2))/(4) &= \\frac{0}{4} \\\\\nr - 2 &= 0 \\\\\nr &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186203860 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $x$, given by\n$2nx + 7 = -4x + 5$\nwhere $n$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $n$?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186203860 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2nx + 7  = -4x + 5\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $n?$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203860 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax = b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $4x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+7 & =-4x+5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =-4x+5+4x \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+4x+7 & =5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7-7 & =5-7 \\\\ 2nx+4x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2n + 4)x  = -2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2n + 4)}_{a}x  = \\underbrace{-2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2n + 4= 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+4 & =0 \\\\ 2n+4-4 & =0-4 \\\\ 2n & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{2n}{2} & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\ n & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203860 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = b$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n$$\nb\\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf $a = 0$, then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax = b$.\n\nWe start by adding $4x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2nx + 7 &= -4x + 5 \\\\\n2nx + 4x + 7 &= -4x + 5 + 4x \\\\\n2nx + 4x + 7 &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2nx + 4x + 7 &= 5 \\\\\n2nx + 4x + 7 - 7 &= 5 - 7 \\\\\n2nx + 4x &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2n + 4)x = -2\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2n + 4)}_{a}x = \\underbrace{-2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2n + 4= 0\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2n + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2n + 4 - 4 &= 0 - 4 \\\\\n2n &= -4 \\\\\n\\frac{2n}{2} &= \\frac{-4}{2} \\\\\nn &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186203884 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $z$, given by\n$(r - 9)z + 2 = -1$\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r$?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186203884 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n(r - 9)z + 2 = -1\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203884 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-9)z+2 & =-1 \\\\ (r-9)z+2-2 & =-1-2 \\\\ {{(r-9)}_{⏟}}_{a}z & ={{-3}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nr - 9 = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r-9 & =0 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186203884 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = b$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n$$\nb\\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf $a = 0$, then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - 9)z + 2 &= -1 \\\\\n(r - 9)z + 2 - 2 &= -1 - 2 \\\\\n(r - 9)_(⏟)_(a)z &= -3_(⏟)_(b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n$r - 9 = 0$.\n\nSolving this for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr - 9 &= 0 \\\\\nr &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186212760 1071 "The following equation is true for all values of $x$.\n\n$mx^2 + 5x + 4 = 2x^2 + 5x + 4$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $m$?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186212760 1071 "The following equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\n$$\nmx^2 + 5x + 4 = 2x^2 + 5x + 4\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $m?$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186212760 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x$, the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the quadratic terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $m$. On the right-hand side, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $2$.\n\nSo, we set\n$m = 2$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186212760 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the quadratic terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $m.$ On the right-hand side, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $2.$\n\nSo, we set\n\n$$\nm = 2.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186212761 1071 "The equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + 12 = 7x^2 + 2x + 12\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $a$ and $b$  are constants. What is the value of $a + b?$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186212761 1071 "The equation\n\n$ax^2 + bx + 12 = 7x^2 + 2x + 12$\nis true for all values of $x$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. What is the value of $a + b$?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186212761 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x$, the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x^2$ on the left-hand side is $a$, and on the right-hand side it is $7$. So, we have\n$a = 7$.\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $b$, and on the right-hand side it is $2$. So, we have\n$b = 2$.\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b &= 7 + 2 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186212761 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x^2$ on the left-hand side is $a,$ and on the right-hand side it is $7.$ So, we have \n\n$$\na = 7.\n$$\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $b,$ and on the right-hand side it is $2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nb = 2.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =7+2 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186212762 1071 "The equation\n\n$$\nx^2 + 9x + 18 = (x + 3)(x + k)\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k?$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186212762 1071 "The equation\n$x^2 + 9x + 18 = (x + 3)(x + k)$\nis true for all values of $x$, where $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186212762 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x$, the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the factored form on the right-hand side must expand to match the left-hand side.\n\nLet's expand the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)(x + k) &= x^{2} + kx + 3x + 3k \\\\\n&= x^{2} + (k + 3)x + 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the expanded form to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 9x + 18 = x^2 + (k + 3)x + 3k\n$$\n\nEquating the constant terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18 &= 3k \\\\\n6 &= k \\\\\nk &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that value $k = 6$ produces the correct linear coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk + 3 &= 6 + 3 \\\\\n&= 9✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side. So, our solution is valid." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186212762 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the factored form on the right-hand side must expand to match the left-hand side.\n\nLet's expand the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x+k) & ={x}^{2}+kx+3x+3k \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(k+3)x+3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the expanded form to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 9x + 18 = x^2 + (k + 3)x + 3k\n$$\n\nEquating the constant terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =3k \\\\ 6 & =k \\\\ k & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that value $k = 6$ produces the correct linear coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+3 & =6+3 \\\\  & =9\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side. So, our solution is valid." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186216983 1071 "Plot the curve of $y=8^{x+2}-4$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186216983 1071 "Plot the curve of $y=8^{x+2}-4.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186216983 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the graph of $y=8^x$.\n\n![](images/b4/b4d4c39839113d060215b8660886aece5f1d825bf849aee72381b84168ee9f8c.png)\n\n- To plot $y=8^{x+2}$, we shift the graph of $y=8^{x}$ horizontally by $2$ units to the left. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-2,1)$, but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](images/aa/aa831d056ebf71397d4153de957289cb815faeb1f5730c6db9546f2a29a4cc4f.png)\n\n- To plot $y=8^{x+2}-4$, we shift the graph of $y=8^{x+2}$ vertically by $4$ units down. The point $(-2,1)$ moves to $(-2,-3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-4$.\n\n![](images/06/0658340d3dc848575da1c5bf0d30c2ec2f2fca0e7db1633c5274059eed16f3cd.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186216983 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the graph of $y=8^x.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4d4c39839113d060215b8660886aece5f1d825bf849aee72381b84168ee9f8c.png)\n\n- To plot $y=8^{x+2},$ we shift the graph of $y=8^{x}$ horizontally by $2$ units to the left. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-2,1),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/aa/aa831d056ebf71397d4153de957289cb815faeb1f5730c6db9546f2a29a4cc4f.png)\n\n- To plot $y=8^{x+2}-4,$ we shift the graph of $y=8^{x+2}$ vertically by $4$ units down. The point $(-2,1)$ moves to $(-2,-3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-4.$\n\n![](images/06/0658340d3dc848575da1c5bf0d30c2ec2f2fca0e7db1633c5274059eed16f3cd.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186216984 1071 "Sketch the graph of the function $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x-4}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186216984 1071 "Sketch the graph of the function $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x-4}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186216984 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^x$.\n\n![](images/a0/a0bbb2611a949209a964f833eefe4870dc7df948bd88dd01567ddc493046be30.png)\n\n- Horizontally shift it by $4$ units to the right to give $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x-4}$. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(4,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](images/31/31abb3ce0f7149bde6aabd69742bf01483d0c4a173efe6dc9f380b03017b1dc3.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x-4}$. The point $(4,1)$ moves to $(4,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](images/98/9815e40e53ca5ae09915c4c8ce88c75a1ca22bd52fe23e40a3b9bdf0c1051744.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186216984 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^x.$\n\n![](images/a0/a0bbb2611a949209a964f833eefe4870dc7df948bd88dd01567ddc493046be30.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $4$ units to the right to give $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x-4}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(4,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/31/31abb3ce0f7149bde6aabd69742bf01483d0c4a173efe6dc9f380b03017b1dc3.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x-4}.$ The point $(4,1)$ moves to $(4,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/98/9815e40e53ca5ae09915c4c8ce88c75a1ca22bd52fe23e40a3b9bdf0c1051744.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186216985 1071 "Show that $I(x) = \\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ is an integrating factor of the equation\n$(x^2y-y^3) \\,\\textrm{d}x - x^{3} \\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186216985 1071 "Show that $I(x) = \\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ is an integrating factor of the equation \n\n$$\n(x^2y-y^3) \\,\\textrm{d}x - x^{3} \\,\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186216985 1074 "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y] = 0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{xy^{3}}((x^{2}y - y^{3})dx - x^{3}dy) &= 0 \\\\\n(\\frac{x}{y^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x})dx - \\frac{x^{2}}{y^{3}}dy &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y^2}-\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad Q(x,y) =-\\dfrac{x^2}{y^3}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x}) =-\\frac{2x}{y^{3}} \\\\\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\frac{x^{2}}{y^{3}}) =-\\frac{2x}{y^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\dfrac{1}{xy^3}\n$$\n\n$\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186216985 1074 "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y] = 0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x{y}^{3}}(({x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y^2}-\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad Q(x,y) =- \\dfrac{x^2}{y^3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}})=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x)=\\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ $\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186216986 1071 "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n\\left(x + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\left(xy + \\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=x+y^2,\\qquad Q(x,y)=xy+\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186216986 1071 "Consider the inexact differential equation\n$\\left(x + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\left(xy + \\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$.\nIt is known that\n$\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}$\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n$P(x,y)=x+y^2,\\qquad Q(x,y)=xy+\\frac{2}{x}$.\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186216986 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x$.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{\\intx^{-1}dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln x} \\\\\n&= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx[(x + y^{2})dx + (xy + \\frac{2}{x})dy] &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} + xy^{2})_(⏟)_(P^(~))dx + (x^{2}y + 2)_(⏟)_(Q^(~))dy &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad \\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x$.\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y$, which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= \\intP^(~)(x, y)dx + F(y) \\\\\n&= \\int(x^{2} + xy^{2})dx + F(y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + \\frac{x^{2}y^{2}}{2} + F(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y$.\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x$, which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= \\intQ^(~)(x, y)dy + G(x) \\\\\n&= \\int(x^{2}y + 2)dy + G(x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{2}y^{2}}{2} + 2y + G(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186216986 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{({x}^{2}y+2)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad\n\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+2y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186216987 1071 "Find an integrating factor for the following equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nx\\,y' = 4y - 3x\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186216987 1071 "Find an integrating factor for the following equation in differential form.\n$x\\,y' = 4y - 3x$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186216987 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation 