c{∂}{∂x}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{x}(-4-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -\\frac{5}{x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|) \\\\  & ={x}^{-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x) \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) =  I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx^{-5} \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) = 0.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186216993 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- $y' = 4xy^2 - 3y + 1$\n\n- $y' - 2y = (x^2 + 5)y^2 + \\sin(x)$\n\n- $y' = e^xy^3 + y - x$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186216993 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n1. $y' = 4xy^2 - 3y + 1$\n2. $y' - 2y = (x^2 + 5)y^2 + \\sin(x)$\n3. $y' = e^xy^3 + y - x$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186216993 1074 "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' = a(x)y^2 + b(x)y + c(x)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with\n$a(x) = 4x, \\qquad b(x)=-3, \\qquad c(x)=1$.\n- Equation II is a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n$y' = (x^2 + 5)y^2 + \\sin(x) + 2y$.\nThis is a Riccati differential equation with\n$a(x) = x^2 + 5, \\qquad b(x)=2, \\qquad c(x)=\\sin(x)$.\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n$y' = e^xy^3 + y - x$.\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a $y^3$ term.\n\nTherefore, 