nters of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of the Sun and $m_2$ be the mass of Mars. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (6.42\\times {10}^{23})}{(2.28\\times {10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 6.42}{(2.28)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{23}}{({10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =(16.392\\,5…)\\times {10}^{20} \\\\  & =(1.639\\,25…)\\times {10}^{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars is\n$\\Big(1.64 \\times 10^{21}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{N}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186217006 1071 "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(3.0\\times10^3)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.0\\times10^{-4})\\,\\textrm{N}$. If one object has mass $(2.0\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{kg}$, then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186217006 1071 "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(3.0\\times10^3)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.0\\times10^{-4})\\,\\textrm{N}.$ If one object has mass $(2.0\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186217