ion states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of Earth and $m_2$ be the mass of the Moon. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (7.35\\times {10}^{22})}{1.98\\times {10}^{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 7.35}{1.98}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{22}}{{10}^{20}} \\\\  & =(147.816…)\\times {10}^{15} \\\\  & =(14.7816…)\\times {10}^{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{14.7816…\\times {10}^{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14.7816…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{16}} \\\\  & =(3.8446…)\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Earth and the Moon is\n$\\Big(3.84 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{m}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186217008 1071 "Find an integrating factor for the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y = 0, \\qquad y > 0\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186217008 1071 "Find an integrating factor for the following differential equation.\n\n$y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y = 0, \\qquad y > 0$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186217008 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{P} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y) = \\exp\\left({-\\int h(y)\\,\\textrm d y}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^y$.\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = y^2, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xy\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2}) = 2y \\\\\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) = y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) &= \\frac{1}{y^{2}}(2y - y) \\\\\n&= y^{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(y) &= \\exp (\\int - (y^{-1})dy) \\\\\n&= \\exp (-\\ln y) \\\\\n&= y^{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot \\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy\\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y^{-1}\\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = 0}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186217008 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{P} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y) = \\exp\\left({-\\int h(y)\\,\\textrm d y}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^y.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =  y^2, \\qquad Q(x,y) =  xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}(2y-y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -({y}^{-1})\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot \\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy\\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y^{-1}\\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = 0}.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186217009 1071 "$$\nx^2y''-5xy'+\\ln(x)y=0, \\qquad x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186217009 1071 "$x^2y''-5xy'+\\ln(x)y=0, \\qquad x>0$\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186217009 1074 "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x)\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x^2$ to get\n\n$$\ny''-\\frac{5}{x}y'+\\frac{\\ln(x)}{x^2}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of $y'$ is\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{5}{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\frac{5}{x}W(x)}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186217009 1074 "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x^2$ to get\n\n$$\ny''-\\frac{5}{x}y'+\\frac{\\ln(x)}{x^2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\frac{5}{x}W(x)}.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186217010 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+\\tan(x)y'+e^x y=0, \\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=4.$ What is $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)?$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186217010 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y''+\\tan(x)y'+e^x y=0, \\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=4$. What is $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186217010 1074 "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x)\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x)\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW^{′} &= -\\tan (x)W \\\\\n\\frac{dW}{W} &= -\\tan (x)dx \\\\\n\\int\\frac{dW}{W} &= \\int - \\tan (x)dx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid W \\mid &= \\ln (\\cos x) + K \\\\\n\\mid W \\mid &= e^{K}\\cos x \\\\\nW &= C\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(0) &= C\\cos (0) \\\\\n4 &= C \\cdot 1 \\\\\nC &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute\n\n$$\nW\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(\\frac{π}{3}) &= 4\\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186217010 1074 "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ W & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ 4 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186217011 1071 "$$\ny''+4y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty,\\infty).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=-3.$ Use Abel's identity to conclude whether $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent on $\\mathbb{R}.$" 13194139533386 175921