rix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186221180 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0.$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n![](images/9c/9cff385cda8e9a4cac784cb4212fee9ec1e65f48a1ebd05f9af75f71aead66aa.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186221181 1071 "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?\n\n- Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually move away from it.\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close but do not necessarily converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and sometimes converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186221181 1071 "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?\n\n1. Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually move away from it.\n2. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$.\n3. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilib