x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}((4x - y) + (4x_{new} - y_{new})) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(4x + 4x_{new} - y) - \\frac{1}{2}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + \\frac{1}{2}(4x + 4x_{new} - y) - \\frac{1}{2}y_{new} \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{2}(4x + 4x_{new} + y) - \\frac{1}{2}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{2}y_{new} &= \\frac{1}{2}(4x + 4x_{new} + y) \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{3}(4x + 4x_{new} + y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(1)$ and $y(2)$.\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0, \\qquad y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + 1 = 1\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{3}(4x + 4x_{new} + y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(4 \\cdot 0 + 4 \\cdot 1 + 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n&\\approx 1.33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=1, \\qquad y=1.33\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{3}(4x + 4x_{new} + y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(4 \\cdot 1 + 4 \\cdot 2 + 1.33) \\\\\n&\\approx 4.44\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx 1.33, \\qquad y(2) \\approx 4.44\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186221186 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-0}2 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((4x-y)+(4{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(1)$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0, \\qquad y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + 1 = 1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & ≈1.33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=1, \\qquad y=1.33.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2+1.33) \\\\  & ≈4.44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx 1.33,\n\\qquad\ny(2) \\approx 4.44.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true