{16} = 0.015\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186221193 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\frac{0.4}{0.1} = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\nk^{1+1} = 4^2 = 16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $\\Delta x = 0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\n\\tau(0.1) \\approx \\frac{0.24}{16} = 0.015.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186221194 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-2y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, 2]^T.$ What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186221194 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = 3x(t) - 2y(t) + 1; y^{′}(t) = 4x(t) + y(t) - 6$\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, 2]^T$. What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186221194 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/2c/2c994a3f48f4d9e12a179b03538977f72e033c5e539ec6f73c6d4529c1674299.png)\nwhere ![](images/d1/d1aac4dbbbd1d7579a0a03c1440b3140efc18f43d6a8016e867e9419af693675.png)\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/61/6118cf06e6accf9f29665d66c4c4d00e0e7e2eb8bdbd98bd9044ee418422ca59.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/fb/fb0d2631e167027b68263b62af5ba65d91442fdc89d1c16e39d6299f97835307.png)\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186221194 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast\n$$\n\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nu^{′} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(u + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u + \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{u^{′}(t) = 3u(t) - 2v(t); v^{′}(t) = 4u(t) + v(t)\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186221195 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0, \\, -2]^T.$ What system is obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186221195 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = x^{2}(t) + x(t) + y(t) + 2; y^{′}(t) = 5x(t) - y^{2}(t) + 3y(t) + 10$\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0, \\, -2]^T$. What system is obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186221195 1074 "To shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/95/951509e719a40caba7ecc5872d983b6cf83128d77af670c88846d2a06d329d8e.png) and ![](images/a3/a3d02714f52ce42ec3e6d78d9cdd91f4c2ca14a59948f078d93318c94ec5e31a.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/cc/cce8b4b07839490b3be0841f8a4e6e73b69426426504625cedecf738947b046d.png)\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t) = u(t), \\qquad\ny(t) = v(t) - 2, \\qquad\nx'(t) = u'(t), \\qquad\nv'(t) = y'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+(v(t)-2)+2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =5u(t)-(v(t)-2)^{2}+3(v(t)-2)+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-({v}^{2}(t)-4v(t)+4)+3v(t)-6+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186221195 1074 "To shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast\n$$\n\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t) = u(t), \\qquad y(t) = v(t) - 2, \\qquad x'(t) = u'(t), \\qquad v'(t) = y'(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n$x^{′}(t)|= x^{2}(t) + x(t) + y(t) + 2; u^{′}(t)|= u^{2}(t) + u(t) + (v(t) - 2) + 2; = u^{2}(t) + u(t) + v(t)$.\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n$y^{′}(t)|= 5x(t) - y^{2}(t) + 3y(t) + 10; v^{′}(t)|= 5u(t) - (v(t) - 2)^{2} + 3(v(t) - 2) + 10; = 5u(t) - (v^{2}(t) - 4v(t) + 4) + 3v(t) - 6 + 10; = 5u(t) - v^{2}(t) + 7v(t)$.\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{u^{′}(t) = u^{2}(t) + u(t) + v(t); v^{′}(t) = 5u(t) - v^{2}(t) + 7v(t)\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186221196 1071 "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=3x-1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?\n\n1. $y=3x+3$\n2. $y=-x$\n3. $y=3x$\n4. $y=-3x$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186221196 1071 "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=3x-1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?\n\n- $y=3x+3$\n\n- $y=-x$\n\n- $y=3x$\n\n- $y=-3x$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186221196 1074 "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=3x$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186221196 1074 "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=3x.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186221197 1071 "![](images/d9/d9bdce761076544aff166409cc78bdf918c4afc2c47b67e6366e3aabb78087b7.png)\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2,$ shown above. Find the fundamental matrix for the system." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186221197 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2$, shown above. Find the fundamental matrix for the system." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186221197 1074 "Given a linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $\\Phi(t)$ of the system is an $n \\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2 \\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{5t} = \\begin{bmatrix}4e^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}e^{5t} = \\begin{bmatrix}4te^{5t} \\\\ 4e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4e^{5t} & 4te^{5t} \\\\ 0 & 4e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi$ can be written in any order." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186221197 1074 "Given a linear system $\\mathbf{x}' = A \\mathbf{x},$ where $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $\\Phi(t)$ of the system is an $n \\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2 \\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}=[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}=[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n![](images/bb/bb710709a593f8d185cde601fb5082d7f79ed21af824c28949477d2011479d95.png)\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi$ can be written in any order." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186221198 1071 "![](images/81/819e3b9672e898801ef7320c1d80246d218ae89e76b3247c86b642a5f5790400.png)\nConsider the system of linear ODEs $\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x}$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix $\\Phi(t)$ for this system." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186221198 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of linear ODEs $\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x}$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix $\\Phi(t)$ for this system." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186221198 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\mathbf x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t)\n$$\n\ndenoted $\\Phi_0(t)$, is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\quad \\Phi_0(0) = I_n\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the identity matrix.\n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}\n$$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}α_{0} & -7α_{1}t \\\\ 7α_{1}t & α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 7\\textrm{i}t, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = -7\\textrm{i}t\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr((7\\textrm{i})t) &= e^{(7\\textrm{i})t} \\\\\nα_{1}(7\\textrm{i})t + α_{0} &= \\cos (7t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (7t) \\\\\nα_{0} + 7\\textrm{i}α_{1}t &= \\cos (7t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (7t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n{α_{0} = \\cos (7t); 7α_{1}t = \\sin (7t)⇒{α_{0} = \\cos (7t); α_{1} = \\frac{1}{7t}\\sin (7t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nα_{0} &= \\cos (7t) \\\\\n7α_{1}t &= 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{7t}\\sin (7t) \\cdot t \\\\\n&= \\sin (7t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (7t) & -\\sin (7t) \\\\ \\sin (7t) & \\cos (7t)\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186221198 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\mathbf x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t),$ denoted $\\Phi_0(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\quad \\Phi_0(0)  = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that $\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & -7{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 7\\textrm{i}t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2 = -7\\textrm{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((7\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(7\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(7\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+7\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/4d/4d6fd69672291bf4c39d33a04656258b89a500bc039588a9fd27eb10db328cc3.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186221199 1071 "Consider the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$, where $A$ is a constant $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $\\Phi(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation $\\Phi'(t) = A \\, \\Phi(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $\\big[\\mathbf{a}, \\: \\mathbf{b} \\big]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$.*\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\Phi(t) = \\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_2'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = \\big[\\mathbf{x}_1'(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2'(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = \\big[A \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: A \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\