 IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228911 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda = 1$ is the parameter of the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0$, and step size $\\Delta x$?\n\n1. $\\Delta x = 0.5$\n2. $\\Delta x = 0.9$\n3. $\\Delta x = 1.2$\n4. $\\Delta x = 1.8$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda \\Delta x$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228911 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda = 1$ is the parameter of the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0,$ and step size $\\Delta x?$\n\n- $\\Delta x = 0.5$\n\n- $\\Delta x = 0.9$\n\n- $\\Delta x = 1.2$\n\n- $\\Delta x = 1.8$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda \\Delta x.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228911 1074 "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\mid R(λΔx) \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid 1 - λΔx \\mid \\mid < 1\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n$0 < \\lambda \\Delta x < 2$.\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2$.\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda=1$, this region simplifies to\n$0 \\mid < Δx < 2$.\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $\\Delta x = 0.5$ satisfies the inequality,\n- $\\Delta x = 0.9$ satisfies the inequality,\n- $\\Delta x = 1.2$ satisfies the inequality, and\n- $\\Delta x = 1.8$ satisfies the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler's method is stable for all given step sizes.\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\"." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228911 1074 "A numerical method $y_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n$is stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0 < \\lambda \\Delta x < 2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda=1,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <Δx<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $\\Delta x = 0.5$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $\\Delta x = 0.9$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $\\Delta x = 1.2$ satisfies the inequality, and\n\n- $\\Delta x = 1.8$ satisfies the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler's method is stable for all given step sizes.\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\"." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228912 1071 "For which of the following values is the Runge–Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda$ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda\\Delta x = 0.6$\n\n- $\\lambda\\Delta x = 1.4$\n\n- $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.8$\n\n- $\\lambda\\Delta x = 3.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x)\n= 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x\n  + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2}\n  - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6}\n  + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228912 1071 "For which of the following values is the Runge–Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda$ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n1. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 0.6$\n2. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 1.4$\n3. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.8$\n4. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 3.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n$R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228912 1074 "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $\\mid R(\\lambda\\Delta x) \\mid < 1$, is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 0.6$, we have\n$\\begin{vmatrix}R(0.6) & |= & 1 - 0.6 + ((0.6)^{2})/(2) - ((0.6)^{3})/(6) + ((0.6)^{4})/(24)\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid \\frac{2747}{5000} \\mid; = 0.5494 < 1.✓$\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 1.4$, we have\n$\\begin{vmatrix}R(1.4) & |= & 1 - 1.4 + ((1.4)^{2})/(2) - ((1.4)^{3})/(6) + ((1.4)^{4})/(24)\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid \\frac{4241}{15000} \\mid; = 0.282… < 1.✓$\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.8$, we have\n$\\begin{vmatrix}R(2.8) & |= & 1 - 2.8 + ((2.8)^{2})/(2) - ((2.8)^{3})/(6) + ((2.8)^{4})/(24)\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid \\frac{639}{625} \\mid; = 1.0224 > 1.\\cdot$\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 3.3$, we have\n$\\begin{vmatrix}R(3.3) & |= & 1 - 3.3 + ((3.3)^{2})/(2) - ((3.3)^{3})/(6) + ((3.3)^{4})/(24)\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid \\frac{167747}{80000} \\mid; = 2.096… > 1.\\cdot$\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.785\\ldots$, so it is not absolutely stable." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228912 1074 "A numerical method\n$y_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n$\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x)\n= 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x\n  + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2}\n  - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6}\n  + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 0.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.6)| & =|1-0.6+\\frac{(0.6)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.6)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.6)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{2747}{5000}| \\\\  & =0.5494<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 1.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.4)| & =|1-1.4+\\frac{(1.4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1.4)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1.4)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{4241}{15000}| \\\\  & =0.282…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.8)| & =|1-2.8+\\frac{(2.8)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.8)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.8)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{639}{625}| \\\\  & =1.0224>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 3.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.3)| & =|1-3.3+\\frac{(3.3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3.3)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3.3)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{167747}{80000}| \\\\  & =2.096…>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.785\\ldots,$ so it is not absolutely stable." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228913 1071 "Given that $\\displaystyle J(t)=\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x$, calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228913 1071 "Given that $\\displaystyle J(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228913 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on\n\n$$\nR = [a,b]\\times [c,d]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nexists and is continuous on $R$. Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d)$, and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x = \\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute $J'(t)$, where\n\n$$\nJ(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nJ'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(\\cos t+2x)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\cos t + 2x)^{2}] &= 2(\\cos t + 2x) \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\cos t + 2x) \\\\\n&= 2(\\cos t + 2x) \\cdot (-\\sin t) \\\\\n&=-2(\\cos t + 2x)\\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ^{′}(t) &= \\int_{0}^{1} - 2(\\cos t + 2x)\\sin tdx \\\\\n&=-2\\sin t\\int_{0}^{1}(\\cos t + 2x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}(\\cos t + 2x)dx &= \\cos t \\cdot \\left.x\\right|_{0}^{1} + \\left.x^{2}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\cos t + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nJ'(t) =-2\\sin t\\,(\\cos t+1)\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228913 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute $J'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nJ'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{\