he special case where\n\n$$\nf(y') = y'\n$$\n\nthe equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y$, we get\n$y = \\dfrac{e^x}{y' - x^2}$,\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$.\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n$f(y') = \\ln(1+y'), \\qquad g(y') = (y')^2$.\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y$, we get\n$y = y' - \\dfrac{x}{1+y'}$,\nwhich is of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n$f(y') = -\\dfrac{1}{1+y'}, \\qquad g(y') = y'$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228918 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f(y') = y',$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{e^x}{y' - x^2},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y = xf(y') + g(y').$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n\n$$\nf(y') = \\ln(1+y'), \\qquad g(y') = (y')^2.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny = y' - \\dfrac{x}{1+y'},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n\n$$\nf(y') = -\\dfrac{1}{1+y'}, \\qquad g(y') = y'.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228919 1071 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-1\\right)^2 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nIf $y'(x) = t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\left( \\dfrac{2\\ln|t| - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2} \\right) f(t) + g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228919 1071 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$y = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-1\\right)^2 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)$.\n\nIf $y'(x) = t$, then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t))$, where\n$y(t) = \\left(\\dfrac{2\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2} \\right) f(t) + g(t)$\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t)$.\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228919 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'$.\n\nWe're told that, under the substitution $y'(x) = t$, our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(t) &= e^{\\intP(t)dt} \\\\\n&= e^{\\int2/(t - 1)dt} \\\\\n&= e^{2\\ln \\mid t - 1 \\mid} \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln (t - 1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= (t - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(t - 1)^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt} + 2(t - 1)x &= (t - 1)^{2}(1 + t)/(t(1 - t)) \\\\\n(t - 1)^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt} + 2(t - 1)x &= \\frac{1 - t^{2}}{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(x(t - 1)^{2}) &= \\frac{1 - t^{2}}{t} \\\\\n\\int\\frac{d}{dt}(x(t - 1)^{2})dt &= \\int\\frac{1 - t^{2}}{t}dt \\\\\nx(t - 1)^{2} &= \\int\\frac{1}{t} - tdt \\\\\nx(t - 1)^{2} &= \\ln \\mid t\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{t^{2}}{2} + C \\\\ x|= (2\\ln \\mid t\\end{vmatrix}- t^{2} + C)/(2(t - 1)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{2\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2}\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny = x(t-1)^2 + 2t\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= ((2\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t^{2} + C)/(2(t - 1)^{2}))(t - 1)^{2} + 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(t)=(t-1)^2,\\ g(t)=2t\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t) = t^2 +1\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228919 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution $y'(x) = t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =\\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{2\\ln|t| - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny = x(t-1)^2 + 2t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}})(t-1)^{2}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)=(t-1)^2,\\ g(t)=2t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t) = t^2 +1.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228920 1071 "![](images/cf/cf38da5e2aec9fc3957f52abc58fc396686db632561953991a3ca0fc23799ace.png)\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ![](images/1a/1a1e4510a1ea4961976e22504d6be845edffd972cc88dda0fde4608b50be2b09.png) where ![](images/cf/cfc60391cfb7369d109e7d56b89b6bfd1f97e7251aacb637f61c94d24cdb4f00.png)*" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228920 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = 3x(t) + y(t) + 1; y^{′}(t) = 3y(t)$,\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{3t} & te^{3t} \\\\ 0 & e^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$*" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228920 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/80/802292289f84a7d69cfaadd91d88d6a4af9560f618dfae6b553fbca16b63cb96.png)\nwhere ![](images/a8/a851d7868235cdaf8cd6914d21b648b3bd7