Find the functions $P$ and $Q$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228938 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'$.\n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x = x(t)$. We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x$.\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin t + 5t^2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x\\sin t + 5t^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules, and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x\\sin t) + \\frac{d}{dx}(5t^{2}) \\\\\nt &= 2\\sin t\\frac{d}{dx}(x) + 2x\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin t) + 10t\\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\\nt &= 2\\sin t + 2x\\cos t\\frac{dt}{dx} + 10t\\frac{dt}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt - 2\\sin t &= (2x\\cos t + 10t)\\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\\n\\frac{t - 2\\sin t}{2x\\cos t + 10t} &= \\frac{dt}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}=\\dfrac{t-2\\sin t}{2x\\cos t+10t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=\\dfrac{2x\\cos t+10t}{t-2\\sin t}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{2x\\cos t + 10t}{t - 2\\sin t} \\\\\n(t - 2\\sin t)\\frac{dx}{dt} &= 2x\\cos t + 10t \\\\\n(t - 2\\sin t)\\frac{dx}{dt} - 2x\\cos t &= 10t \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} - \\frac{2\\cos t}{t - 2\\sin t}x &= \\frac{10t}{t - 2\\sin t} \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{2\\cos t}{2\\sin t - t}x &= \\frac{10t}{t - 2\\sin t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{2\\cos t}{2\\sin t-t}, \\qquad Q(t) = \\dfrac{10t}{t-2\\sin t}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228938 1074 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x = x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin t + 5t^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules, and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{t}^{2}) \\\\ t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+10t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+10t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}=\\dfrac{t-2\\sin t}{2x\\cos t+10t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=\\dfrac{2x\\cos t+10t}{t-2\\sin t}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\ (t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t \\\\ (t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =10t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}x & =\\frac{10t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t}x & =\\frac{10t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{2\\cos t}{2\\sin t-t}, \\qquad Q(t) = \\dfrac{10t}{t-2\\sin t}.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228939 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- $D_1(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+x\\cos(y)\\,y$\n\n- $D_2(y)=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\sin(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+3y$\n\n- $D_3(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}}+e^{x}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\ln(x)\\,y$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228939 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n1. $D_1(y)=\\dfra