oefficient of the second term is $x\\cos(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator $D_2$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_2(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}, f_1(x) = -\\sin(x),$ and $f_0(x) = 3.$\n\n- Operator $D_3$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_3(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_3(x) = 1, f_2(x) = e^{x}, f_1(x) = 0,$ and $f_0(x) = -\\ln(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228940 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny'' - 2y' - 3y = 0,  \\qquad y'(1) = y'(3) = 0,\\quad 1\\le x\\le3\n$$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n![](images/aa/aa58b04d0ebdd18e4259fd3a1386332043f317f12b94c7b4ad468bef77584a0f.png)\n\nFind the fundamental solutions and use $A$ to determine if the given BVP has a unique or infinitely many solutions." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228940 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y'' - 2y' - 3y = 0, \\qquad y'(1) = y'(3) = 0,\\quad 1\\le x\\le3$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(1) & y_{2}^{′}(1) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(3) & y_{2}^{′}(3)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the fundamental solutions and use $A$ to determine if the given BVP has a unique or infinitely many solutions." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228940 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\ny''(x)+P(x)y'(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_{1}y(a)+\\beta_{1}y'(a)=0,\\qquad\n\\alpha_{2}y(b)+\\beta_{2}y'(b)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha_i, \\beta_i$ are real constants such that $(\\alpha_i,\\beta_i)\\ne(0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$  \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n![](images/ac/ac7be07071df654f4d2fec18b6b93afb21fd13169e7890ea091b496d7f9b206c.png)\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y\\equiv0$ if and only if $\\det(A)\\ne0.$\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions.  So, setting $\\alpha_i=0,$ $\\beta_i=1,$ $a=1,$ and $b=3,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n![](images/91/91f85685d5a17221398842923709c20b0e296e53fedb30ee6a4fdcb16d99e0d8.png)\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y=e^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2m-3 & =0 \\\\ (m-3)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_{1}(x)=e^{3x}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;y_{2}(x)=e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2'(x) = -e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3(1)} & -{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3(3)} & -{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3} & -{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{9} & -{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(3{e}^{3})(-{e}^{-3})-(-{e}^{-1})(3{e}^{9}) \\\\  & =-3+3{e}^{8} \\\\  & =3({e}^{8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)\\ne0,$ we conclude that this BVP has the unique trivial solution $y\\equiv0.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228940 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\ny''(x)+P(x)y'(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_{1}y(a)+\\beta_{1}y'(a)=0,\\qquad \\alpha_{2}y(b)+\\beta_{2}y'(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b]$, and $\\alpha_i, \\beta_i$ are real constants such that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i,\\beta_i)\\ne(0,0)\n$$\n\nFurthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$.\n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}α_{1}y_{1}(a) + β_{1}y_{1}^{′}(a) & α_{1}y_{2}(a) + β_{1}y_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ α_{2}y_{1}(b) + β_{2}y_{1}^{′}(b) & α_{2}y_{2}(b) + β_{2}y_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y\\equiv0$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\det(A)\\ne0\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting $\\alpha_i=0$, $\\beta_i=1$, $a=1$, and $b=3$, the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(1) & y_{2}^{′}(1) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(3) & y_{2}^{′}(3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y=e^{mx}$. The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} - 2m - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(m - 3)(m + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1$, and so the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_{1}(x)=e^{3x}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;y_{2}(x)=e^{-x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2'(x) = -e^{-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{3(1)} & -e^{-1} \\\\ 3e^{3(3)} & -e^{-3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{3} & -e^{-1} \\\\ 3e^{9} & -e^{-3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) &= (3e^{3})(-e^{-3}) - (-e^{-1})(3e^{9}) \\\\\n&=-3 + 3e^{8} \\\\\n&= 3 (e 8 - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\det(A)\\ne0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that this BVP has the unique trivial solution $y\\equiv0$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228941 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = x^3 e^{x},  \\qquad y'(0) = y'(\\pi) = 0,\\quad 0\\leq x \\leq \\pi\n$$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n![](images/b5/b580aad5cc5933b519879a940442764e414a74e3ad3018e3b1f48191484a0f78.png)\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\quad$ The absolute value of $\\det(A)$ is ![](images/9d/9d59df7aed836f589b9744d0e1e594ef38e567fc17402deaad196a1b6768db09.png)\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP  ![](images/47/471ff03c04cc9f360469d4c2f5f2e02ac091d211d7f6830b379997143dbd2ea1.png)\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, our inhomogeneous BVP  ![](images/ad/ad34113aed97995e76ef24368fdce7b5c2b6cddc1678615e47b3ab61e04603bd.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228941 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y'' + 16y = x^3 e^{x}, \\qquad y'(0) = y'(\\pi) = 0,\\quad 0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(0) & y_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(π) & y_{2}^{′}(π)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\quad$ The absolute value of $\\det(A)$ is $AAAAA$\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP $AAAAAAAAA$\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, our inhomogeneous BVP $AAAAAAAAAAAA$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228941 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \n\\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\nwhere $P, Q,$ and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha_i$ and $\\beta_i$ are real constants such that $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ \n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ Specifically:\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y \\equiv 0,$ then the inhomogeneous problem either has no solution or a unique solution.\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous problem either has infinitely many solutions or no solution.\n\nTo classify the solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n![](images/f0/f0ae121481e21c471d2e7b7eab072a2de438456dc76ac909db13b0262234fb80.png)\n\nThe homogeneous BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$ if and only if $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$ In other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the homogeneous BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting $\\alpha_i = 0, \\beta_i = 1, a = 0,$ and $b = \\pi,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n![](images/f0/f0cdf9d9e9f668853a863704ae8f72a6acd8842ffefff70a46e1d826f8b8a510.png)\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m = \\pm 4\\textrm i.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 4x, \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = -4\\sin 4x, \\qquad y_2'(x) = 4\\cos 4x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\pi ) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\pi )\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|0\\cdot 4-4\\cdot 0| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A) = 0,$ the corresponding homogeneous BVP $\\textrm{has infinitely many solutions}.$\n\nTherefore, since the corresponding homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, our inhomogeneous BVP $\\textrm{has infinitely many solutions or no solution}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228941 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\nwhere $P, Q$, and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b]$, and $\\alpha_i$ and $\\beta_i$ are real constants such that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$. Specifically:\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y \\equiv 0$, then the inhomogeneous problem either has no solution or a unique solution.\n- If the homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous problem either has infinitely many solutions or no solution.\n\nTo classify the solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}α_{1}y_{1}(a) + β_{1}y_{1}^{′}(a) & α_{1}y_{2}(a) + β_{1}y_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ α_{2}y_{1}(b) + β_{2}y_{1}^{′}(b) & α_{2}y_{2}(b) + β_{2}y_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe homogeneous BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\det(A) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the homogeneous BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting\n\n$$\n\\alpha_i = 0, \\beta_i = 1, a = 0\n$$\n\nand $b = \\pi$, the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(0) & y_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(π) & y_{2}^{′}(π)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set\n\n$$\ny = e^{m x}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} + 16 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nm = \\pm 4\\textrm i\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 4x, \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 4x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = -4\\sin 4x, \\qquad y_2'(x) = 4\\cos 4x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}-4\\sin (4 \\cdot 0) & 4\\cos (4 \\cdot 0) \\\\ -4\\sin (4 \\cdot π) & 4\\cos (4 \\cdot π)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}det(A) & &= & 0 \\cdot 4 - 4 \\cdot 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 0\n$$\n\nthe corresponding homogeneous BVP $\\textrm{has infinitely many solutions}$.\n\nTherefore, since the corresponding homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, our inhomogeneous BVP $\\textrm{has infinitely many solutions or no solution}$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228942 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\ny'' = 0, \\qquad y(4) = 0, \\quad y(3) + y'(3) = 0,\\quad 3\\leq x \\leq 4\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228942 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y'' = 0, \\qquad y(4) = 0, \\quad y(3) + y'(3) = 0,\\quad 3\\leq x \\leq 4$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228942 1074 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n\n$$\ny''(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny' = A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(4) = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A + B = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad B = -4A.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny = Ax - 4A = A(x-4).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(3) + y'(3) = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-A+A = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y = A\\cdot \\left(x-4\\right),$\nwhere $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228942 1074 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n$y''(x) = 0$.\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n$y' = A$.\n\nApplying the condition $y(4) = 0$, we have\n\n$$\n4A + B = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad B = -4A\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny = Ax - 4A = A(x-4)\n$$\n\nApplying the condition\n\n$$\ny(3) + y'(3) = 0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n-A+A = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cdot \\left(x-4\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228945 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic extensions of some functions from $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with the Fourier series shown above.  What are the Fourier constant coefficient, the $3\\textrm{rd}$ Fourier sine coefficient, and the $8\\textrm{th}$ Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228945 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}f(x) & ∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{c_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)c_{n}\\cos (nx) + d_{n}\\sin (nx)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic extensions of some functions from $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with the Fourier series shown above. What are the Fourier constant coefficient, the $3\\textrm{rd}$ Fourier sine coefficient, and the $8\\textrm{th}$ Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$?" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228945 1074 "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x) + \\beta g(x)\n\\sim\n\\frac{\\alpha a_0 + \\beta c_0}{2}\n+\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\n\\Big(\n(\\alpha a_n + \\beta c_n)\\cos(nx)\n+\n(\\alpha b_n + \\beta d_n)\\sin(nx)\n\\Big).\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ are\n\n- $-3a_0+4c_0$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- $-3a_n+4c_n,$ $n \\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-3b_n+4d_n,$ $n \\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the Fourier constant coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_0+4c_0$,\n\n- the $3$rd Fourier sine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3b_3+4d_3$, and\n\n- the $8$th Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_8+4c_8$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228945 1074 "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(x) & ∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{c_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(c_{n}\\cos (nx) + d_{n}\\sin (nx))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x) + \\beta g(x) \\sim \\frac{\\alpha a_0 + \\beta c_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\Big((\\alpha a_n + \\beta c_n)\\cos(nx) + (\\alpha b_n + \\beta d_n)\\sin(nx) \\Big)\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ are\n\n- $-3a_0+4c_0$ for the constant coefficient,\n- $-3a_n+4c_n$, $n \\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n- $-3b_n+4d_n$, $n \\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the Fourier constant coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_0+4c_0$,\n- the $3$rd Fourier sine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3b_3+4d_3$, and\n- the $8$th Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_8+4c_8$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228946 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\cos(2x)$ on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$. Complete the statements below by filling in the blanks.\nDetermine if the function $f(x)$ is odd or even, and which terms of the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228946 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\cos(2x)$ on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$ Complete the statements below by filling in the blanks.\nDetermine if the function $f(x)$ is odd or even, and which terms of the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228946 1074 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-x) &= (-x)\\cos (2(-x)) \\\\\n&= (-x)\\cos (-2x) \\\\\n&= (-x)\\cos (2x) \\\\\n&=-f(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x)\n$$\n\nwe have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi]$, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\cos(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi,\\pi]$ is $0$. Thus, $a_n=0$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\ge1\n$$\n\nand $a_0=0$.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{odd}}$ and the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{cosine}}$ terms in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228946 1074 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x) = -f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\cos(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi,\\pi]$ is $0.$ Thus, $a_n=0$ for all $n\\ge1,$ and $a_0=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{odd}}$ and the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{cosine}}$ terms  in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228947 1071 "![](images/23/238174bfcd0e07603a8516f9271efff876e36cf8021403be57b049ec31b60977.png)\nLet ![](images/a6/a600da93d54ed8b4857befa58c1165e8c02b77d1c2fc5b1f460961dc135c50a7.png) be the ![](images/18/1898332940848e9192873afe50ec9d9a005baf8bcb86ce77c66231321ba3fe0e.png)-periodic extension of a function from ![](images/5f/5ff1e4e25630b889a7d37b5ac7252742b9b0f0abdf659ece1f3b0700815ddc44.png) to ![](images/d3/d33b55bb712e2f8437446299936998a79ee357a6aa77ce6c31932bb464868bb8.png) with the Fourier series shown above. What is the ![](images/db/db700c8ee08453d9f4b0f347c4f88daf4186495494840905e9196b2243899653.png)th sine Fourier coefficient of ![](images/c3/c38b7489bfb362de743d5a53941eaddfc382f5d50756b13bc6a4fda893a692e9.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] 