5 false] [17592186228948 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\pi]$.\n\n${x + 2,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ 5 - x,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228948 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\pi].$\n\n![](images/93/93b55a42a534e4f547b1cab1b700d96b4d5b51656f2090673eb051fefe44221d.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228948 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^-}f(x) = +\\infty, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^+}f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi].$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ except at the point $x=1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^-}h(x) = 3, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^+}h(x) = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228948 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ are infinite:\n$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^-}f(x) = +\\infty, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^+}f(x) = -\\infty$\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$.\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ except at the point $x=1$, where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^-}h(x) = 3, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^+}h(x) = 4$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228949 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s) = \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$, where $f(t)$ is given by\n${e^{2t},\\begin{vmatrix}t \\le 3 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}t > 3$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228949 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s) = \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n![](images/e7/e70e54b6a70cebd480b86baf9d0e1d90bde0b3d9277c982d5d5e77218d0d2f5d.png)" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228949 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= F(s) = \\int_{0}^{∞}f(t)e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function\n\n$$\n{e^{2t},\\begin{vmatrix}t \\le 3 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}t > 3\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\int_{0}^{∞}f(t)e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{3}e^{2t} \\cdot e^{-st}dt + \\int_{3}^{∞}1 \\cdot e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{3}e^{2t - st}dt + \\int_{3}^{∞}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{3}e^{(2 - s)t}dt + \\int_{3}^{∞}e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if\n\n$$\ns \\leq 0\n$$\n\nFor $s > 0$, we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\int_{0}^{3}e^{(2 - s)t}dt + \\int_{3}^{∞}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2 - s}[e^{(2 - s)t}]_{0}^{3} + \\int_{3}^{∞}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) + lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{3}^{a}e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) + \\left.lim_(a → ∞)(\\frac{-e^{-st}}{s})\\right|_{3}^{a} \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) - \\frac{1}{s} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)(e^{-as} - e^{-3s}) \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) - \\frac{1}{s} \\cdot (0 - e^{-3s}) \\\\\n&= (e^{3(2 - s)} - 1)/(2 - s) + \\frac{e^{-3s}}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 0$." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228949 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n![](images/48/48965cee8a09df4f637b4d997fdb9c90673e69a90ea1320434f9b471a2fed280.png)\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{2t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{(2-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s \\leq 0.$\n\nFor $s > 0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{(2-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-s}{[{e}^{(2-s)t}]}_{0}^{3}+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{-{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 0.$" 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228950 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty)$.\n\n$f(x)|= {1,\\begin{vmatrix}⌈x⌉is even \\\\ -1,\\end{vmatrix}⌈x⌉is odd; g(x)|= u(x - 1) + u(x - \\frac{1}{2}) + u(x - \\frac{1}{3}) + ⋯; h(x)|= ⌈\\frac{x}{2}⌉$\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $\\lceil x \\rceil$ is the ceiling function." 13194139533386 17592186045585 false] [17592186228950 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & =u(x-1)+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{3})+⋯ \\\\ h(x) & =⌈\\frac{x}{2}⌉\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $\\lceil x \\rceil$ is the ceiling function." 13194139533386 17592186045585 true] [17592186228950 1074 "Suppose $I\\subseteq \\mathbb R$. A function $f: I \\to \\mathbb R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]\\subset I$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty)$. The function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,2,3,\\ldots$, and any closed interval $[a,b]\\subset [0,\\infty)$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/3c/3ce7aa18de332c3eb1bc57b6bf7c50acf1573717d642c731a18a85f4f9fb813a.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty)$. Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,1]$. Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,\\dfrac12,\\dfrac13,\\dfrac14,\\ldots$, so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty)$. The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2,4,\\ldots$, and any closed interval $[a,b]\\subse